00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:00,670
-Everybody skip!

264
00:10:00,670 --> 00:10:02,890
-But I betcha they're in
 league with Unga Khan.

265
00:10:02,890 --> 00:10:03,520
-Unga Khan.

266
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:04,500
-Is that Matt Dillon?

267
00:10:07,070 --> 00:10:07,570
-Hi!

268
00:10:07,570 --> 00:10:08,970
-Which one of us
 is he waving at?

269
00:10:12,890 --> 00:10:15,340
-Follow me!

270
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:17,700
-Oh, it's Jim Henson's
 Birth of a Nation babies.

271
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:20,660
-Oh.

272
00:10:20,660 --> 00:10:21,640
-(MOCKINGLY) Follow me!

273
00:10:25,100 --> 00:10:27,010
-Look at all those
 horses under the sea.

274
00:10:27,010 --> 00:10:28,640
-So how do they get
 them under the sea?

275
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:29,900
-Sea horses?

276
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:30,400
-D'oh!

277
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:33,090
Oh!

278
00:10:33,090 --> 00:10:34,000
-Ben Hur.

279
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:34,620
-Ben Hecht.

280
00:10:34,620 --> 00:10:35,900
-Charles MacArthur.

281
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:37,680
-Jimmy Smits.

282
00:10:37,680 --> 00:10:38,840
-Ah, here's the stop.

283
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:39,710
Everybody get transferred.

284
00:10:42,510 --> 00:10:44,380
-[HUMS ALONG WITH
 BACKGROUND MUSIC]

285
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:46,870
-OK, if things get tough,
 we'll offer 'em Billy, agreed?

286
00:10:46,870 --> 00:10:47,370
-Cool.

287
00:10:47,370 --> 00:10:47,870
-Agreed.

288
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:54,760
-Oh, it's like the end of
 a high speed chase in LA.

289
00:10:54,760 --> 00:10:56,970
-Ride back to the
 Sacred City and report

290
00:10:56,970 --> 00:10:58,440
to Sharad, High
 Priest of Atlantis,

291
00:10:58,440 --> 00:11:00,790
that we are returning
 with strange prisoners.

292
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:01,760
-Weird prisoners.

293
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:10,580
-(SHRILLY) I wanna
 ride with Diana!

294
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:12,720
-Uh, you're a little late
 for the posse, fella!

295
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:16,780
-(SIMULTANEOUSLY)
 Da da-da-da-da,

296
00:11:16,780 --> 00:11:20,440
da-da-da, da-da-da-da da.

297
00:11:20,440 --> 00:11:22,940
-The end.

298
00:11:22,940 --> 00:11:24,230
-Oh.

299
00:11:24,230 --> 00:11:26,200
-With Mark Spitz,
 Jimmy Smits, and Rossy,

300
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:27,570
the French wonder horse.

301
00:11:27,570 --> 00:11:28,220
-Open the gate!

302
00:11:28,220 --> 00:11:29,150
-It's Martos.

303
00:11:29,150 --> 00:11:32,470
-Open the gate and make
 some summer sausage, quick!

304
00:11:32,470 --> 00:11:33,870
-Too late.

305
00:11:33,870 --> 00:11:36,720
-Ouch.

306
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:37,700
-Open the back door!

307
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:40,090
He can't stop!

308
00:11:40,090 --> 00:11:42,910
-Here, wash and wax this.

309
00:11:42,910 --> 00:11:44,100
-Thank you, sir.

310
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:47,730
Ah, jeez, all I got is a crummy
 popcorn ball and some Jujubes.

311
00:11:47,730 --> 00:11:50,310
-Looks like a set The
 Monkeys would end up on.

312
00:11:53,570 --> 00:11:54,750
-Confident, dry, and secure.

313
00:11:54,750 --> 00:11:57,580
-Darius approaches the
 city with strange captives.

314
00:11:57,580 --> 00:11:58,970
-Strange captives?

315
00:11:58,970 --> 00:11:59,500
-Weird captives.

316
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:00,000
Kept

317
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:00,680
-Where are they?

318
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:03,800
-We believe them to be
 agents of Unga Khan.

319
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:04,550
-Unga Khan.

320
00:12:04,550 --> 00:12:05,530
-That sucks.

321
00:12:05,530 --> 00:12:07,710
-There will be no
 peace in Atlantis.

322
00:12:07,710 --> 00:12:09,770
-Can we break your hat open
 now and eat the popcorn?

323
00:12:09,770 --> 00:12:14,360
-Power of this evil
 usurper, Unga Khan.

324
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:17,050
-Anderson Windows
 and Wall-- Doors.

325
00:12:17,050 --> 00:12:19,250
-Before we could return
 and capture the strangers,

326
00:12:19,250 --> 00:12:21,370
the Volkites appeared
 and destroyed them

327
00:12:21,370 --> 00:12:22,670
with their atom guns.

328
00:12:22,670 --> 00:12:24,360
-I wanted them alive!

329
00:12:24,360 --> 00:12:25,790
-They are alive.

330
00:12:25,790 --> 00:12:27,500
-Well then everybody's happy.

331
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:28,800
-Well then, shall we eat?

332
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:29,940
There's Dutch loaf.

333
00:12:29,940 --> 00:12:31,790
-Sharad's men have captured
 them, Your Majesty,

334
00:12:31,790 --> 00:12:33,630
and they're heading
 for the Sacred City!

335
00:12:33,630 --> 00:12:34,880
-Enough TV for you two.

336
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:36,010
Now get outside and play!

337
00:12:36,010 --> 00:12:38,070
-Recapture the
 prisoners at any cost.

338
00:12:38,070 --> 00:12:38,570
You

339
00:12:38,570 --> 00:12:39,070
-Hi-keeba.

340
00:12:43,370 --> 00:12:45,340
-Oh! [IMITATES SOUND
 OF HEADS BUMPING ROCK]

341
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:46,260
-Owie!

342
00:12:46,260 --> 00:12:46,910
-Oh, my head.

343
00:12:46,910 --> 00:12:47,410
-My head.

344
00:12:47,410 --> 00:12:49,240
-Oh, watch out in back!

345
00:12:49,240 --> 00:12:51,590
-Ow!

346
00:12:51,590 --> 00:12:52,690
[HUM ALONG WITH
 BACKGROUND MUSIC]

347
00:13:00,850 --> 00:13:01,350
-Oh, look.

348
00:13:01,350 --> 00:13:03,390
It's the three horsemen--
 no, the four horsemen-- no,

349
00:13:03,390 --> 00:13:05,160
the several horsemen
 of the apocalypse.

350
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:09,600
-Oh, Mother Superior
 jumped the gun.

351
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:10,100
-Nope.

352
00:13:14,790 --> 00:13:15,750
-Whooaaa!

353
00:13:15,750 --> 00:13:19,010
Big hill, everybody!

354
00:13:19,010 --> 00:13:20,720
-Well, what do you know?

355
00:13:20,720 --> 00:13:27,290
[THEME MUSIC]

356
00:13:27,290 --> 00:13:28,570
-Ooh.

357
00:13:28,570 --> 00:13:30,540
-The Dick Clark story on ABC.

358
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:36,520
-He any relationship
 to Dick Cheney?

359
00:13:36,520 --> 00:13:37,880
-I don't think so.

360
00:13:37,880 --> 00:13:39,160
-Oh.

361
00:13:39,160 --> 00:13:41,130
-Marion?

362
00:13:41,130 --> 00:13:46,990
-TVs Marion from Happy Days.

363
00:13:46,990 --> 00:13:50,050
-It sounds like the
 soundtrack is drunk.

364
00:13:50,050 --> 00:13:56,940
[EXAGGERATEDLY IMITATES MUSIC]

365
00:13:56,940 --> 00:14:00,990
-Oh, Ted Fleshman, Jr., John
 Russell, Jr., Lon Chaney, Jr.,

366
00:14:00,990 --> 00:14:03,590
Sammy Davis, Jr., Junior
 Samples, Junior Absorbing, Jr.

367
00:14:03,590 --> 00:14:05,000
-Lots of juniors.

368
00:14:09,550 --> 00:14:11,780
-(TOGETHER) Vy
 Russell in flight.

369
00:14:11,780 --> 00:14:14,700
[BUZZES] Afternoon
 delight-- aww.

370
00:14:14,700 --> 00:14:16,140
-It's cute, huh?

371
00:14:16,140 --> 00:14:17,790
-Pollexfen, for
 your "denfentures".

372
00:14:17,790 --> 00:14:18,460
-Den-then-ten-tures.

373
00:14:22,540 --> 00:14:23,830
-All of justice?

374
00:14:23,830 --> 00:14:25,840
That's kind of a gross
 exaggeration, isn't it?

375
00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:26,810
-It's not a court of justice.

376
00:14:26,810 --> 00:14:27,750
It's a court of law.

377
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:32,930
-Who is Detective Division?

378
00:14:32,930 --> 00:14:33,990
-Hmm.

379
00:14:33,990 --> 00:14:35,710
-I'm Lieutenant Dick Chasen.

380
00:14:35,710 --> 00:14:36,680
-He's eating breakfast.

381
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:39,420
-This hadn't been
 a routine case.

382
00:14:39,420 --> 00:14:42,070
And to find the answer, I've had
 to put a lot of little pieces

383
00:14:42,070 --> 00:14:42,820
together.

384
00:14:42,820 --> 00:14:44,110
-It's parents' day at Attica!

385
00:14:44,110 --> 00:14:46,970
--most cases can
 be called closed.

386
00:14:46,970 --> 00:14:49,740
It was the day before the
 Butcher was to be executed.

387
00:14:49,740 --> 00:14:51,770
-He was visited by his attorney
 Paul Lowe in the death-house.

388
00:14:51,770 --> 00:14:52,440
-San Quentin Crisp.

389
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:54,420
Cute.

390
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:56,420
-It was at this first
 meeting the Butcher Benton

391
00:14:56,420 --> 00:14:58,020
case took its first switch.

392
00:14:58,020 --> 00:15:00,270
-Jimmy Smits.

393
00:15:00,270 --> 00:15:01,720
-One last tip, Butcher.

394
00:15:01,720 --> 00:15:03,650
The evidence against
 you is so strong,

395
00:15:03,650 --> 00:15:05,040
the government turned
 down your appeal.

396
00:15:05,040 --> 00:15:06,440
-Aww, they still
 think your father's

397
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:07,980
more talented than you are.

398
00:15:07,980 --> 00:15:09,560
-You started rough,
 and now you're

399
00:15:09,560 --> 00:15:11,880
still trying to throw me curves.

400
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:13,250
-Look, I don't blame
 you for being edgy,

401
00:15:13,250 --> 00:15:14,700
but get this straight.

402
00:15:14,700 --> 00:15:16,690
I didn't double cross you.

403
00:15:16,690 --> 00:15:18,330
I never worked
 harder for a client.

404
00:15:18,330 --> 00:15:20,080
-You mean you never
 worked harder for a client

405
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:20,800
to get him sentenced.

406
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:21,300
-Oh.

407
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:22,300
-Oh, that hurt.

408
00:15:22,300 --> 00:15:24,370
-You're a fool, Butcher.

409
00:15:24,370 --> 00:15:26,990
If you hadn't tried to double
 cross Squeamy Ellis and Joe

410
00:15:26,990 --> 00:15:28,760
Marcellia, they wouldn't
 have turned state's evidence

411
00:15:28,760 --> 00:15:29,390
against you.

412
00:15:29,390 --> 00:15:29,970
-Touche.

413
00:15:29,970 --> 00:15:30,870
-But you had to get greedy.

414
00:15:30,870 --> 00:15:34,220
You wanted to keep the
 whole $600,000 for yourself.

415
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:35,200
-Which one's in jail?

416
00:15:35,200 --> 00:15:35,700
-Hmm.

417
00:15:35,700 --> 00:15:37,510
-Boys got sore, and
 I don't blame them.

418
00:15:37,510 --> 00:15:38,900
-It was all your idea.

419
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:40,710
You planned the whole job.

420
00:15:40,710 --> 00:15:41,970
You hired us.

421
00:15:41,970 --> 00:15:43,610
When you found out
 I'd staked the money,

422
00:15:43,610 --> 00:15:45,910
you decided it was
 time for me to die.

423
00:15:45,910 --> 00:15:47,410
-Shh, Mom's here.

424
00:15:47,410 --> 00:15:50,080
-You got those two crumbs to
 turn state's evidence on me.

425
00:15:50,080 --> 00:15:52,460
You stinking rotten mouthpiece.

426
00:15:52,460 --> 00:15:55,770
-That's Mr. Stinking
 Rotten Mouthpiece to you.

427
00:15:55,770 --> 00:15:58,920
-Now look, what's the sense
 in not giving me the money?

428
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:00,170
It's not gonna do you any good.

429
00:16:00,170 --> 00:16:00,670
-Oh yeah.

430
00:16:00,670 --> 00:16:01,170
Yeah.

431
00:16:01,170 --> 00:16:02,370
-It's locked up in a CD.

432
00:16:02,370 --> 00:16:04,720
-I'll have the satisfaction
 of knowing that none of you

433
00:16:04,720 --> 00:16:06,540
three crumbs are gonna spend it.

434
00:16:06,540 --> 00:16:07,870
-What about Eva?

435
00:16:07,870 --> 00:16:10,020
-Don't you owe her something?

436
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:11,550
You tell me where
 the money is, I'll

437
00:16:11,550 --> 00:16:13,560
see that she gets your share.

438
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:16,010
-I've got a different idea.

439
00:16:16,010 --> 00:16:19,170
I'm going to kill you
 and Squeamy and Joe.

440
00:16:19,170 --> 00:16:20,910
Then I'll take
 care of Eva myself.

441
00:16:20,910 --> 00:16:21,780
-Neat.

442
00:16:21,780 --> 00:16:25,240
-You thick-headed ape,
 you're gonna die tomorrow.

443
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:26,060
-Oh, that's right.

444
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:28,390
I stand corrected.

445
00:16:28,390 --> 00:16:31,130
-Remember what I said.

446
00:16:31,130 --> 00:16:31,920
I'm gonna getcha.

447
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:32,420
-Sucker.

448
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:33,420
-All three of ya.

449
00:16:33,420 --> 00:16:35,780
-Wait, there's only one of me.

450
00:16:35,780 --> 00:16:39,110
-Even for you, Butcher,
 that'd be quite a trick.

451
00:16:39,110 --> 00:16:41,370
So long, dead man.

452
00:16:41,370 --> 00:16:43,390
-OK, see you late--
 [SPUTTERS] Hey!

453
00:16:43,390 --> 00:16:45,690
-Remember what I said.

454
00:16:45,690 --> 00:16:47,070
I'm gonna kill you.

455
00:16:47,070 --> 00:16:48,730
All three of you.

456
00:16:48,730 --> 00:16:51,770
-The butcher, the baker,
 and the candlestick maker.

457
00:16:51,770 --> 00:16:54,020
-The thief, the
 cook, the wife, and--

458
00:16:54,020 --> 00:16:55,490
-Whoa-ho, Centerville.

459
00:16:55,490 --> 00:16:58,780
Real great place to
 bring your kids up.

460
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:03,940
-French dips.

461
00:17:03,940 --> 00:17:06,290
-Back in Los Angeles, two
 men sat in a bar room,

462
00:17:06,290 --> 00:17:08,660
listening to a newscaster
 tell his headline story.

463
00:17:08,660 --> 00:17:10,100
-Oh, I've heard this joke.

464
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:11,330
-The Butcher's
 threatened to kill

465
00:17:11,330 --> 00:17:11,940
Paul Lowe, Squeamy
 Ellis, and Joe Marcellia.

466
00:17:11,940 --> 00:17:12,440
-Listerine?

467
00:17:12,440 --> 00:17:14,610
[EXAGGERATED CHEWING SOUNDS]

468
00:17:14,610 --> 00:17:17,710
-For them having turned
 state's evidence against him.

469
00:17:17,710 --> 00:17:21,230
-Squeamy Ellis didn't seem
 happy over the Butcher's threat.

470
00:17:21,230 --> 00:17:23,520
-(MOCKING) They're not happy
 with the Butcher's threat!

471
00:17:23,520 --> 00:17:26,910
-But they had figured, once a
 dead man, always a dead man.

472
00:17:26,910 --> 00:17:27,410
-That's smart.

473
00:17:27,410 --> 00:17:28,330
-Like a boy scout.

474
00:17:28,330 --> 00:17:28,830
-Ooh!

475
00:17:28,830 --> 00:17:31,790
-This was one time everyone was
 in for a surprise, including

476
00:17:31,790 --> 00:17:33,450
the entire police department.

477
00:17:33,450 --> 00:17:35,890
-Mhm, mhm.

478
00:17:35,890 --> 00:17:37,250
Go on.

479
00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:37,750
Come on.

480
00:17:37,750 --> 00:17:41,200
-Hmm, wonder what Billy's
 doing in Family Circus today.

481
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:41,830
-Wow.

482
00:17:41,830 --> 00:17:43,820
Prime cuts go on sale at noon.

483
00:17:43,820 --> 00:17:45,270
-Building code under fire.

484
00:17:49,630 --> 00:17:51,090
-[GASPS] Oh.

485
00:17:51,090 --> 00:17:53,540
-You see the headlines, Captain?

486
00:17:53,540 --> 00:17:55,670
-Teletype just came
 in from San Francisco.

487
00:17:55,670 --> 00:17:57,200
-Here's the receipt
 for your paper.

488
00:17:57,200 --> 00:17:59,920
-Set for 5 o'clock
 this afternoon.

489
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:02,590
And like the newspapers
 say, he still

490
00:18:02,590 --> 00:18:04,390
refuses to talk about
 that stolen money.

491
00:18:04,390 --> 00:18:06,320
-(MUTTERED) Still refuses to
 talk about that stolen money.

492
00:18:06,320 --> 00:18:08,520
-Well, I still have hopes
 of coming up with something.

493
00:18:08,520 --> 00:18:10,090
-Ooh, he sat on the pen set!

494
00:18:10,090 --> 00:18:12,380
-That's what I
 dropped by for, Dick.

495
00:18:12,380 --> 00:18:14,650
Butcher's execution, the
 department's marking his case

496
00:18:14,650 --> 00:18:15,370
closed.

497
00:18:15,370 --> 00:18:17,410
That means you'll be reassigned.

498
00:18:17,410 --> 00:18:18,600
-Hmm.

499
00:18:18,600 --> 00:18:21,470
-I spent a whole year
 trying to break this case.

500
00:18:21,470 --> 00:18:23,110
As far as I'm concerned,
 Butcher's death

501
00:18:23,110 --> 00:18:24,550
is just the beginning.

502
00:18:24,550 --> 00:18:26,240
-Could you reassign me
 with Pepper Anderson?

503
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:30,020
-I can't prove it, but I think
 that Paul Lowe was the top man.

504
00:18:30,020 --> 00:18:33,420
And even though I have to keep
 working on it while off duty,

505
00:18:33,420 --> 00:18:36,440
I'm going to get Lowe, Squeamy
 Ellis, and Joe Marcellia.

506
00:18:36,440 --> 00:18:36,940
-Hmm.

507
00:18:36,940 --> 00:18:37,810
-OK, Lieutenant.

508
00:18:37,810 --> 00:18:39,070
-Hey, hey, hey!

509
00:18:39,070 --> 00:18:40,540
-Officially, I've
 taken you off the case.

510
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:41,040
-Touch, touch.

511
00:18:41,040 --> 00:18:42,140
-Unofficially, I wish you luck.

512
00:18:42,140 --> 00:18:44,300
-Hey, you forgot to
 pat his head, too.

513
00:18:44,300 --> 00:18:45,050
-Do me a favor.

514
00:18:45,050 --> 00:18:46,280
Keep me posted.

515
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:46,870
-Will do.

516
00:18:46,870 --> 00:18:47,870
-Wait, isn't this your office?

517
00:18:47,870 --> 00:18:48,470
-Thanks, John.

518
00:18:51,940 --> 00:18:53,670
I headed for Eva Martin.

519
00:18:53,670 --> 00:18:55,190
-But then, who didn't? [PANTS]

520
00:18:55,190 --> 00:18:57,370
-She worked in the
 local burlesque house.

521
00:18:57,370 --> 00:19:00,790
I'm a cop, and my job is to
 ask questions and get answers.

522
00:19:00,790 --> 00:19:01,610
-All right, talk!

523
00:19:01,610 --> 00:19:04,700
-So I decided I might as
 well start again with her.

524
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:06,150
-Da-dum, da-dum, da-dum.

525
00:19:06,150 --> 00:19:08,850
-And I walked
 really, really fast.

526
00:19:08,850 --> 00:19:09,350
Morning!

527
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:14,380
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) Either
 those curtains go, or I do.

528
00:19:14,380 --> 00:19:16,340
-Come in.

529
00:19:16,340 --> 00:19:17,250
-Oh, hi.

530
00:19:17,250 --> 00:19:18,260
I saw your sister outside.

531
00:19:18,260 --> 00:19:20,650
You know, the quiet one?

532
00:19:20,650 --> 00:19:21,630
-What do you want?

533
00:19:21,630 --> 00:19:23,290
-I'd like to talk to you.

534
00:19:23,290 --> 00:19:24,690
-Talk?

535
00:19:24,690 --> 00:19:26,340
You're a cop, aren't you?

536
00:19:26,340 --> 00:19:28,020
-That's a good question.

537
00:19:28,020 --> 00:19:29,870
I can't even find a
 three-ton armored car,

538
00:19:29,870 --> 00:19:31,970
let alone the $600,000.

539
00:19:31,970 --> 00:19:33,740
I've been on the case a year.

540
00:19:33,740 --> 00:19:36,580
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 Well you're a crappy cop.

541
00:19:36,580 --> 00:19:38,740
Look, it's 9:00 AM and
 I'm dressed like this.

542
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:39,650
Leave me alone!
 -Look, I told you.

543
00:19:39,650 --> 00:19:40,920
I don't know anything
 about the money.

544
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:41,920
I never did.

545
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:43,420
-Benton's gonna be
 dead in a little while.

546
00:19:43,420 --> 00:19:45,140
I thought it might
 make a difference.

547
00:19:45,140 --> 00:19:47,730
-I never knew anything
 about the money!

548
00:19:47,730 --> 00:19:48,570
-Money, you know?

549
00:19:48,570 --> 00:19:51,400
Medium exchange,
 storehouse of value, green.

550
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:55,230
-I didn't even know Charles was
 in the rackets until the trial.

551
00:19:55,230 --> 00:19:57,130
-Now take it easy, kid.

552
00:19:57,130 --> 00:19:58,560
-You're on, Eva!

553
00:19:58,560 --> 00:20:00,150
-Hey, it's Al Jolson.

554
00:20:00,150 --> 00:20:02,990
-What's he doing out there?

555
00:20:02,990 --> 00:20:04,810
-So, uh, can I wear
 your clothes now?

556
00:20:08,050 --> 00:20:13,010
-Pff, boy, that burlesque cow
 sure puts on a good brunch.

557
00:20:13,010 --> 00:20:14,730
-It's a Jack Ruby planet.

558
00:20:14,730 --> 00:20:18,230
-The Jack Ruby program!

559
00:20:18,230 --> 00:20:20,110
-Oh, damn.

560
00:20:20,110 --> 00:20:22,460
It's my evil twin.

561
00:20:22,460 --> 00:20:23,900
-Well, let's see here.

562
00:20:23,900 --> 00:20:26,490
GWM seeks leatherman for-- oh.

563
00:20:29,650 --> 00:20:31,580
-He's wearing espadrilles.

564
00:20:31,580 --> 00:20:33,110
Oh, even criminals
 have to punch in.

565
00:20:33,110 --> 00:20:35,750
What do you know?

566
00:20:35,750 --> 00:20:37,700
-Ha, now I can drink.

567
00:20:37,700 --> 00:20:40,780
-Honey, here's your wrap.

568
00:20:40,780 --> 00:20:42,310
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 Thanks, Mr. Lithgow.

569
00:20:42,310 --> 00:20:44,860
-Anytime.

570
00:20:44,860 --> 00:20:46,960
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 New stuff killed-- oh, hi.

571
00:20:54,280 --> 00:20:56,740
-Oh, mommy.

572
00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:58,940
-It's his mother?

573
00:20:58,940 --> 00:21:00,020
-What a dump.

574
00:21:00,020 --> 00:21:01,390
Who said that?

575
00:21:01,390 --> 00:21:03,130
-Two months ago, Butcher
 Benton, without revealing

576
00:21:03,130 --> 00:21:06,580
the whereabouts of the $600,000
 stolen in the armored car

577
00:21:06,580 --> 00:21:09,000
hold-up, paid for his
 crimes against society

578
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:10,750
in the gas chamber
 at San Quentin.

579
00:21:10,750 --> 00:21:13,360
-Walter Winchell is
 hiding behind the curtain.

580
00:21:13,360 --> 00:21:14,230
-There.

581
00:21:14,230 --> 00:21:16,090
Jeez, I hate Howard Stern.

582
00:21:16,090 --> 00:21:17,500
-Come on, baby.

583
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:19,750
Don't waste tears on Benton.

584
00:21:19,750 --> 00:21:22,630
-I feel so badly about Charles.

585
00:21:22,630 --> 00:21:26,600
If I'd known, maybe I
 could've done something.

586
00:21:26,600 --> 00:21:27,460
-How about a drink?

587
00:21:27,460 --> 00:21:29,400
We could both use it.

588
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:31,800
-An artist can't drink
 while she's working.

589
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:33,080
-Then how about a karab shake?

590
00:21:38,390 --> 00:21:42,270
-Well, I've got blanch
 peanuts here, if-- hmm.

591
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:46,930
-It's from Ed McMahon.

592
00:21:46,930 --> 00:21:48,420
-Well now what's the matter?

593
00:21:48,420 --> 00:21:50,240
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 They spelled my name wrong.

594
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:52,320
-It's a message from Charles.

595
00:21:52,320 --> 00:21:56,560
He told me to open
 it if-- he died.

596
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:57,060
-[WHIMPERS]

597
00:21:57,060 --> 00:21:58,940
-Finale, Eva.

598
00:21:58,940 --> 00:21:59,950
-All right.

599
00:21:59,950 --> 00:22:02,130
-Jolson again.

600
00:22:02,130 --> 00:22:04,770
-How about a steak
 after the show?

601
00:22:04,770 --> 00:22:08,660
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 Oh, he always offers me beef.

602
00:22:08,660 --> 00:22:11,170
-Dumb, you stupid
 dumb-- the idiot!

603
00:22:11,170 --> 00:22:14,310
What is with me today?

604
00:22:14,310 --> 00:22:15,830
-Hmm.

605
00:22:15,830 --> 00:22:18,430
Well it says here, "Dear Eva,
 don't eat too much steak.

606
00:22:18,430 --> 00:22:19,170
Love, Charles."

607
00:22:19,170 --> 00:22:20,050
-Hmm.

608
00:22:20,050 --> 00:22:22,510
-Opening the letter the
 Butcher are given Eva,

609
00:22:22,510 --> 00:22:25,070
found the map outlining a
 section of the city sewer

610
00:22:25,070 --> 00:22:25,970
system.

611
00:22:25,970 --> 00:22:26,830
-It's his varicose veins.

612
00:22:26,830 --> 00:22:28,100
-Clearly identified
 was the spot where

613
00:22:28,100 --> 00:22:30,840
the Butcher had hidden
 the stolen payroll.

614
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:32,480
He replaced it with a $50 bill.

615
00:22:32,480 --> 00:22:33,650
-And a pork chop.

616
00:22:33,650 --> 00:22:37,350
-I guess he figured $50
 to gain $600,000 was not

617
00:22:37,350 --> 00:22:38,420
a bad day's work.

618
00:22:38,420 --> 00:22:40,570
-No one will be seated during
 the frightening letter-folding

619
00:22:40,570 --> 00:22:41,070
scene.

620
00:22:41,070 --> 00:22:42,380
-Put it in a mutual fund.

621
00:22:45,850 --> 00:22:53,770
[DOORS CLOSING]

622
00:22:53,770 --> 00:22:57,160
-Welcome to the Satellite
 of Love's annual Any Excuse

623
00:22:57,160 --> 00:22:58,790
for a Parade parade.

624
00:22:58,790 --> 00:23:01,440
This year, we salute
 The Undersea Kingdom.

625
00:23:01,440 --> 00:23:03,540
I'm your host, Alan
 Thicke, and my co-host

626
00:23:03,540 --> 00:23:05,950
down on the parade
 route is Mary Frann.

627
00:23:05,950 --> 00:23:06,530
-Mary Frann?

628
00:23:06,530 --> 00:23:08,290
You said I could
 be Dick Van Patten.

629
00:23:08,290 --> 00:23:09,620
-Oh, sure thing, Dick.

630
00:23:09,620 --> 00:23:11,900
-OK, our first float
 is turning the corner

631
00:23:11,900 --> 00:23:14,340
onto Gypsy Rose Lane.

632
00:23:14,340 --> 00:23:17,050
Gypsy, as you know, is
 this year's grand marshal.

633
00:23:17,050 --> 00:23:18,510
-Always an honor!

634
00:23:18,510 --> 00:23:21,440
-This float is a tribute
 to Ray "Crash" Corrigan.

635
00:23:21,440 --> 00:23:23,580
-And it's a good thing
 Crash isn't driving.

636
00:23:23,580 --> 00:23:24,900
-Ho ho, that's right, Dick.

637
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:26,700
Hey, look, there's little Billy!

638
00:23:26,700 --> 00:23:28,540
-Hurry, Diane.

639
00:23:28,540 --> 00:23:31,660
-That float is sponsored by
 the American Dairy Association

640
00:23:31,660 --> 00:23:34,120
and is made entirely of
 recycled milk cartons.

641
00:23:34,120 --> 00:23:36,340
-Dairy, you don't
 find it underwater,

642
00:23:36,340 --> 00:23:37,450
but it's got water in it.

643
00:23:37,450 --> 00:23:41,270
The ADA inviting you to enjoy
 dairy in all its permutations

644
00:23:41,270 --> 00:23:43,020
at least three times a day.

645
00:23:43,020 --> 00:23:44,980
-Here comes the
 Florida Institute

646
00:23:44,980 --> 00:23:48,230
of Marine Biology marching
 band, playing the popular love

647
00:23:48,230 --> 00:23:50,140
theme from The Undersea Kingdom.

648
00:23:50,140 --> 00:23:52,590
[GARBLED MUSIC]

649
00:23:52,590 --> 00:23:57,230
-And here's a perennial
 favorite, the lovable Tom Servo

650
00:23:57,230 --> 00:23:59,880
from TV's Mystery
 Science Theater 3,000.

651
00:23:59,880 --> 00:24:02,460
He portrays Dr.
 Norton's submarine from?

652
00:24:02,460 --> 00:24:03,380
-The Undersea Kingdom!

653
00:24:03,380 --> 00:24:04,100
-That's right.

654
00:24:04,100 --> 00:24:04,770
-Did you know, Alan?

655
00:24:04,770 --> 00:24:07,720
It took 18 seamstresses working
 round the clock three months

656
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:09,570
to bring the plucky bot to life.

657
00:24:09,570 --> 00:24:10,800
-In his balloon!

658
00:24:10,800 --> 00:24:11,300
-Mhm.

659
00:24:11,300 --> 00:24:15,430
-And Tom is filled with over
 15,000 cubic yards of hydrogen.

660
00:24:15,430 --> 00:24:16,910
-I think you mean helium, Dick.

661
00:24:16,910 --> 00:24:19,410
-Following Servo is the American
 Pepper Association float.

662
00:24:19,410 --> 00:24:21,220
Hats off to pepper.

663
00:24:21,220 --> 00:24:22,050
-Uh, pepper.

664
00:24:22,050 --> 00:24:23,960
Up your day with pepper!

665
00:24:23,960 --> 00:24:25,570
-The American Pepper
 Association invites

666
00:24:25,570 --> 00:24:27,180
you to try pepper
 as a side dish.

667
00:24:27,180 --> 00:24:29,260
-You know, pepper was used
 in the miniature sequences

668
00:24:29,260 --> 00:24:32,470
in The Undersea Kingdom to
 simulate the ocean floor.

669
00:24:32,470 --> 00:24:33,730
-But pepper.

670
00:24:33,730 --> 00:24:34,540
[INHALING RAPIDLY]

671
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:35,510
-Look out!

672
00:24:35,510 --> 00:24:36,960
[SCREAMS]

673
00:24:36,960 --> 00:24:38,900
-Achoo!

674
00:24:38,900 --> 00:24:40,360
-(DEADPAN) Oh, no.

675
00:24:40,360 --> 00:24:42,550
Servo has burst into flames.

676
00:24:42,550 --> 00:24:43,820
People are running around.

677
00:24:43,820 --> 00:24:44,390
Fire is shooting.

678
00:24:44,390 --> 00:24:45,750
Oh, oh, the humanity.

679
00:24:45,750 --> 00:24:48,020
Uh, people are
 running and screaming.

680
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:50,310
Uh, we've got commercial
 sign, and I'm just

681
00:24:50,310 --> 00:24:52,650
gonna step into
 this doorway here.

682
00:24:52,650 --> 00:24:58,580
[THEME MUSIC]

683
00:24:58,580 --> 00:24:59,410
-Two hundred miles--

684
00:24:59,410 --> 00:25:00,320
-[COUGHING]

685
00:25:00,320 --> 00:25:02,540
-San Francisco, the case
 took another switch.

686
00:25:02,540 --> 00:25:03,040
Which--

687
00:25:03,040 --> 00:25:03,540
-Jimmy Smits.

688
00:25:03,540 --> 00:25:06,230
--turned the next 72 hours into
 one long, hideous nightmare.

689
00:25:06,230 --> 00:25:07,830
-My in-laws came to visit.

690
00:25:07,830 --> 00:25:08,990
-[GUFFAWS]

691
00:25:08,990 --> 00:25:11,350
-Biochemist was
 making preparations

692
00:25:11,350 --> 00:25:13,340
for a final experiment
 which he hoped

693
00:25:13,340 --> 00:25:16,310
would lead the way
 to a cure for cancer.

694
00:25:16,310 --> 00:25:18,600
He'd been successful
 with laboratory animals.

695
00:25:18,600 --> 00:25:19,220
-Well, who hasn't?

696
00:25:19,220 --> 00:25:21,710
-The last step called
 for a human body.

697
00:25:21,710 --> 00:25:22,740
-That's where I came in.

698
00:25:22,740 --> 00:25:24,460
-Bradshaw's assistant
 quickly made a deal

699
00:25:24,460 --> 00:25:27,040
with the local mortuary and
 returned to the laboratory

700
00:25:27,040 --> 00:25:28,970
with the dead body
 of Butcher Benton.

701
00:25:28,970 --> 00:25:32,020
-Then he hired the winner of
 the Joe Flynn look-alike contest

702
00:25:32,020 --> 00:25:33,380
to come on down.

703
00:25:33,380 --> 00:25:35,180
-(GERMAN ACCENT) S'all
 right under the sheet?

704
00:25:35,180 --> 00:25:38,350
S'all right.

705
00:25:38,350 --> 00:25:39,110
-You made good time.

706
00:25:39,110 --> 00:25:40,680
-Well, I didn't waste any.

707
00:25:40,680 --> 00:25:41,350
-Any trouble?

708
00:25:41,350 --> 00:25:41,850
-McHale?

709
00:25:41,850 --> 00:25:43,790
-No, I just handed the money
 over and moved the body

710
00:25:43,790 --> 00:25:46,070
into your station wagon,
 and that was that.

711
00:25:46,070 --> 00:25:46,570
-Good.

712
00:25:46,570 --> 00:25:47,550
-(NASALLY VOICE) This is fun.

713
00:25:52,940 --> 00:25:54,020
-Checking him for change.

714
00:25:54,020 --> 00:25:55,110
-He's in good shape.

715
00:25:55,110 --> 00:25:55,950
-Well, I hope so.

716
00:25:55,950 --> 00:25:57,120
-For a dead man.

717
00:25:59,850 --> 00:26:01,410
-We'll get other
 blood samples ready,

718
00:26:01,410 --> 00:26:02,800
then we'll run this
 one through fast.

719
00:26:02,800 --> 00:26:03,770
-Right.

720
00:26:03,770 --> 00:26:07,590
-But, uh, first
 get junior samples.

721
00:26:07,590 --> 00:26:09,920
-Now wheel him into the
 machine and then raise him.

722
00:26:09,920 --> 00:26:11,880
-Raise with a good
 Midwestern value.

723
00:26:11,880 --> 00:26:14,620
Loyalty, hard work, you know.

724
00:26:14,620 --> 00:26:16,830
-A little late for a CAT scan.

725
00:26:21,370 --> 00:26:24,670
-We will sell no
 wine before its time.

726
00:26:24,670 --> 00:26:26,660
-(BABY TALK) Ooh, he's a
 hungry little fella, there.

727
00:26:26,660 --> 00:26:28,090
Isn't he a little hungry fella?

728
00:26:32,510 --> 00:26:33,010
-McHale?

729
00:26:36,780 --> 00:26:37,740
-Elvis!

730
00:26:37,740 --> 00:26:38,750
-Ready?

731
00:26:38,750 --> 00:26:41,890
-You mean you're going to
 give him 287,000 volts?

732
00:26:41,890 --> 00:26:43,270
-Yes, yes.

733
00:26:43,270 --> 00:26:44,900
-OK, you're the boss.

734
00:26:44,900 --> 00:26:47,510
-We'll check for that
 before we start dissecting.

735
00:26:47,510 --> 00:26:49,560
Turn off the lights.

736
00:26:49,560 --> 00:26:53,330
-We need a dramatic effect.

737
00:26:53,330 --> 00:26:55,700
-He just invented
 the Lite-Brite!

738
00:26:55,700 --> 00:26:56,850
-Hmm.

739
00:26:56,850 --> 00:26:57,350
-Ribs!

740
00:26:57,350 --> 00:26:58,940
That's what I'm hungry for!

741
00:26:58,940 --> 00:26:59,800
-Mmm.

742
00:26:59,800 --> 00:27:02,260
-Old boy, if you
 respond properly

743
00:27:02,260 --> 00:27:06,670
and my theory is sound, you'll
 be more famous dead than alive.

744
00:27:06,670 --> 00:27:08,980
-Like most artists.

745
00:27:08,980 --> 00:27:11,600
-Jimmy Smits.

746
00:27:11,600 --> 00:27:16,280
[ELECTRIC BUZZING]

747
00:27:16,280 --> 00:27:16,810
-It's alive!

748
00:27:16,810 --> 00:27:17,310
It's--

749
00:27:17,310 --> 00:27:17,810
-No.

750
00:27:17,810 --> 00:27:18,310
-Oh.

751
00:27:18,310 --> 00:27:20,950
Aw.

752
00:27:20,950 --> 00:27:22,400
-Bond meter.

753
00:27:22,400 --> 00:27:23,830
-Oh.

754
00:27:23,830 --> 00:27:28,670
-(SINGING) GE, we bring
 Lon Chaney to life!

755
00:27:28,670 --> 00:27:30,140
-Inspector Henderson
 and Captain Binghamton,

756
00:27:30,140 --> 00:27:32,180
working together
 for your future!

757
00:27:32,180 --> 00:27:33,560
-I think it needs
 the electric light

758
00:27:33,560 --> 00:27:35,500
orchestra to bring
 it all together.

759
00:27:35,500 --> 00:27:38,020
-El Dorado, El
 Dorado, El Dorado.

760
00:27:40,860 --> 00:27:42,710
-You see, it goes
 all the way to 11.

761
00:27:48,790 --> 00:27:51,920
-Oh, gee, get a garbage
 bag and a squeegee.

762
00:27:51,920 --> 00:27:52,890
--rotation of the part.

763
00:27:57,240 --> 00:27:58,630
-(SINGING) Old man, liver.

764
00:27:58,630 --> 00:28:01,590
-Mr. Bradshaw, he's
 beginning to breathe.

765
00:28:01,590 --> 00:28:03,360
You've brought this
 man back to life!

766
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:04,890
-With Coast!

767
00:28:04,890 --> 00:28:06,460
-The heart muscle
 simply responded

768
00:28:06,460 --> 00:28:08,770
to a terrific electrical
 shock, that's all.

769
00:28:08,770 --> 00:28:10,680
-Really?

770
00:28:10,680 --> 00:28:13,890
-Yes, but it does prove that
 the cells still function.

771
00:28:13,890 --> 00:28:15,120
-So, lunch?

772
00:28:15,120 --> 00:28:16,780
-Dr. Bradshaw--

773
00:28:16,780 --> 00:28:17,550
-Heal the child within!

774
00:28:17,550 --> 00:28:19,040
-He is alive.

775
00:28:19,040 --> 00:28:21,540
-The shock reaction,
 it can't last.

776
00:28:21,540 --> 00:28:25,380
-His cells are multiplying
 now, if your theory is right.

777
00:28:25,380 --> 00:28:30,810
-If we could keep that
 reaction going longer-- but

778
00:28:30,810 --> 00:28:33,490
it's worth a try.

779
00:28:33,490 --> 00:28:35,560
-Get the body out.

780
00:28:35,560 --> 00:28:37,770
-How come I always have
 to get the body out?

781
00:28:41,960 --> 00:28:43,270
-Coffee?

782
00:28:43,270 --> 00:28:45,390
-Come on, get up
 for school, faker.

783
00:28:45,390 --> 00:28:46,250
Come on, up and at 'em.

784
00:28:46,250 --> 00:28:46,750
Rise and shine.

785
00:28:46,750 --> 00:28:47,670
Come on.

786
00:28:51,750 --> 00:28:55,580
-Oh, jeez, I've seen calf
 liver cuter than him.

787
00:28:55,580 --> 00:28:57,560
-Get some adrenaline
 from the supply room.

788
00:28:57,560 --> 00:28:58,820
Some anti-nitrite too.

789
00:28:58,820 --> 00:29:00,090
Hurry, hurry!

790
00:29:00,090 --> 00:29:01,990
-100% pure adrenaline.

791
00:29:01,990 --> 00:29:05,440
-(WHISPERED) Point break.

792
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:07,280
-Oh, boy.

793
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:10,460
Did I get electrocuted
 last night?

794
00:29:10,460 --> 00:29:17,790
[WHEEZING SOUND EFFECT]

795
00:29:17,790 --> 00:29:19,190
-Senor Wences lives!

796
00:29:23,730 --> 00:29:25,770
-[GROANS] What am I smelling?

797
00:29:25,770 --> 00:29:28,200
Did somebody die in
 here?-- oh, I did!

798
00:29:31,330 --> 00:29:32,310
-Uh, oh.

799
00:29:32,310 --> 00:29:35,560
Oh, did I-- did I go
 home with you last night?

800
00:29:35,560 --> 00:29:37,150
Oh.

801
00:29:37,150 --> 00:29:38,570
-Can I have a glass of water?

802
00:29:38,570 --> 00:29:41,210
I'm really parched.

803
00:29:41,210 --> 00:29:42,190
-[GRUNTS]

804
00:29:42,190 --> 00:29:44,630
-Hey, now.

805
00:29:44,630 --> 00:29:46,090
-[GRUNTS]

806
00:29:46,090 --> 00:29:48,050
-Your heart can't
 stand the strain.

807
00:29:48,050 --> 00:29:49,510
-[GRUNTS]

808
00:29:49,510 --> 00:29:50,970
-Now you must lie still.

809
00:29:50,970 --> 00:29:53,410
Please, please.

810
00:29:53,410 --> 00:29:54,900
-Look, my back
 teeth are floating.

811
00:29:54,900 --> 00:29:58,060
I've got to go to the bathroom.

812
00:29:58,060 --> 00:30:00,500
-He's got a saggy
 diaper that leaks.

813
00:30:00,500 --> 00:30:02,550
-Yuck.

814
00:30:02,550 --> 00:30:06,190
-You're right, I
 had that coming.

815
00:30:06,190 --> 00:30:08,970
-Uh, you think people
 come back from the dead?

816
00:30:08,970 --> 00:30:11,090
You should see this lab
 around quitting time.

817
00:30:11,090 --> 00:30:12,840
[GUFFAWING]

818
00:30:12,840 --> 00:30:14,460
-Back to the Butcher.

819
00:30:14,460 --> 00:30:17,070
He tried to talk, but
 the electrical voltage

820
00:30:17,070 --> 00:30:18,790
had burned out his vocal cords.

821
00:30:18,790 --> 00:30:20,010
-Thanks for everything, bye now.

822
00:30:20,010 --> 00:30:22,090
-Hadn't destroyed his brain.

823
00:30:22,090 --> 00:30:23,610
He knew who he was.

824
00:30:23,610 --> 00:30:26,530
He knew who he hated.

825
00:30:26,530 --> 00:30:27,550
-I'm in here!

826
00:30:27,550 --> 00:30:30,060
-Hey, use the damn stairs!

827
00:30:30,060 --> 00:30:33,200
-Kill Lowe, Squeamy,
 and Joe Marcellia.

828
00:30:33,200 --> 00:30:33,890
-Hey!

829
00:30:33,890 --> 00:30:34,560
Wait-- oh.

830
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:36,870
-The electrical voltage that Dr.
 Bradshaw had given the Butcher

831
00:30:36,870 --> 00:30:38,820
had increased his
 cellular structure

832
00:30:38,820 --> 00:30:40,850
to the point where he
 was no longer a man.

833
00:30:40,850 --> 00:30:42,700
-But made for a woman.

834
00:30:42,700 --> 00:30:43,970
-Dr. Bradshaw's
 experiment had created

835
00:30:43,970 --> 00:30:47,540
a vicious, brutal animal with
 an almost inconceivable amount

836
00:30:47,540 --> 00:30:48,490
of strength.

837
00:30:48,490 --> 00:30:49,790
-So he joined the LA
 police department.

838
00:30:49,790 --> 00:30:52,250
-Dr. Bradshaw must have believed
 the Butcher's regained life

839
00:30:52,250 --> 00:30:54,150
would last for
 only a few minutes.

840
00:30:54,150 --> 00:30:54,720
-If refrigerated.

841
00:30:54,720 --> 00:30:57,020
-Therefore, instead
 of calling for help,

842
00:30:57,020 --> 00:31:00,350
the doctor decided to
 handle his creation alone.

843
00:31:00,350 --> 00:31:01,900
-Now, easy Benton.

844
00:31:01,900 --> 00:31:02,570
Just relax.

845
00:31:02,570 --> 00:31:04,240
-You relax, I'm dead.

846
00:31:04,240 --> 00:31:06,150
--doctor, and I want
 to try to help you.

847
00:31:06,150 --> 00:31:07,430
Do you understand me?

848
00:31:07,430 --> 00:31:09,140
You're in my laboratory.

849
00:31:09,140 --> 00:31:11,600
-You're Milton,
 my brand new son.

850
00:31:11,600 --> 00:31:12,520
-You were declared dead.

851
00:31:12,520 --> 00:31:13,230
-Hmm.

852
00:31:13,230 --> 00:31:15,350
-I tried it an experiment on you
 and brought you back to life,

853
00:31:15,350 --> 00:31:16,780
although I didn't intend that.

854
00:31:16,780 --> 00:31:17,280
-Uh-huh.

855
00:31:17,280 --> 00:31:18,460
-Now, you've had a great shock.

856
00:31:18,460 --> 00:31:20,700
Now, now come and sit down.

857
00:31:20,700 --> 00:31:21,930
Be quiet, now.

858
00:31:21,930 --> 00:31:22,530
Come and sit down.

859
00:31:22,530 --> 00:31:24,440
-C'mere, I'll fix
 you a nice Coke.

860
00:31:24,440 --> 00:31:27,730
-Be quiet, he tells him?

861
00:31:27,730 --> 00:31:28,230
-McHale?

862
00:31:28,230 --> 00:31:29,430
Oh.

863
00:31:29,430 --> 00:31:33,680
I can't wait until
 I'm in the boat next.

864
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:34,180
-Careful.

865
00:31:34,180 --> 00:31:35,550
His reactions are violent.

866
00:31:35,550 --> 00:31:36,720
-How do you explain this?

867
00:31:36,720 --> 00:31:37,860
-Ah, his reactions are violent.

868
00:31:37,860 --> 00:31:40,680
-100 times, perhaps thousands.

869
00:31:40,680 --> 00:31:41,810
His strength is unbelievable.

870
00:31:41,810 --> 00:31:42,930
-Hm.

871
00:31:42,930 --> 00:31:43,430
Gum?

872
00:31:43,430 --> 00:31:45,620
-No, it's too late
 for the ammo nitrate.

873
00:31:45,620 --> 00:31:47,580
I want to get some
 blood samples,

874
00:31:47,580 --> 00:31:49,630
make some tests on him,
 find out what's happened.

875
00:31:49,630 --> 00:31:51,190
Now get me a syringe.

876
00:31:51,190 --> 00:31:52,060
-Yes, sir!

877
00:31:55,220 --> 00:31:56,720
-Good, now get me a sarong.

878
00:32:02,420 --> 00:32:05,040
-Hm?

879
00:32:05,040 --> 00:32:06,810
-Relax, the first one's free.

880
00:32:06,810 --> 00:32:08,070
-What the--?

881
00:32:08,070 --> 00:32:09,140
Oh, I get it.

882
00:32:09,140 --> 00:32:10,910
He's indestructible.

883
00:32:10,910 --> 00:32:12,150
-(IN UNISON) Ohhh.

884
00:32:12,150 --> 00:32:14,710
-You bent my needle,
 you freaked out maniac!

885
00:32:14,710 --> 00:32:17,800
-Why won't the needle
 penetrate the skin?

886
00:32:17,800 --> 00:32:21,910
-The tissue must be nearly
 a solid mass of cells.

887
00:32:21,910 --> 00:32:23,210
-How're we gonna get
 the blood samples?

888
00:32:23,210 --> 00:32:24,680
-Molting is very glamorous.

889
00:32:24,680 --> 00:32:26,830
-Surgically, perhaps.

890
00:32:26,830 --> 00:32:27,650
-Surgically?

891
00:32:27,650 --> 00:32:31,580
Wait, I want a second opinion.

892
00:32:31,580 --> 00:32:32,460
-Take it easy.

893
00:32:32,460 --> 00:32:33,990
You can't leave here now.

894
00:32:33,990 --> 00:32:36,030
I'm responsible for
 your being alive.

895
00:32:36,030 --> 00:32:36,660
[STRANGLING SOUNDS]

896
00:32:36,660 --> 00:32:37,970
-You're not the boss of me.

897
00:32:37,970 --> 00:32:40,230
[CHUCKLES]

898
00:32:40,230 --> 00:32:45,640
-You know, I really cannot
 tell which is more fun to kill.

899
00:32:45,640 --> 00:32:46,680
Huh, it's a draw.

900
00:32:50,930 --> 00:32:52,320
-Huh.

901
00:32:52,320 --> 00:32:53,850
Well, done and done.

902
00:32:53,850 --> 00:32:55,260
Killed two birds with one stone.

903
00:32:58,370 --> 00:33:00,900
Sorry, guys.

904
00:33:00,900 --> 00:33:03,490
Uh, say, you know if there's
 a breakfast place near-- oh,

905
00:33:03,490 --> 00:33:04,270
that's right.

906
00:33:04,270 --> 00:33:04,770
You're dead.

907
00:33:04,770 --> 00:33:06,040
Sorry, I forgot.

908
00:33:06,040 --> 00:33:08,060
-(SINGING) This will
 be the day that I die.

909
00:33:08,060 --> 00:33:10,360
(SPEAKING) I finally understand
 the lyrics to that "American

910
00:33:10,360 --> 00:33:12,540
Pie." (RESUMES SINGING)
 Drove my Chevy--

911
00:33:12,540 --> 00:33:19,980
[THEME MUSIC]

912
00:33:19,980 --> 00:33:23,000
-Well, he's wandered
 into a Mr. Bulky's.

913
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:24,130
-Oh, let's see here.

914
00:33:24,130 --> 00:33:27,370
Licorice whips, Jujubes,
 Slo-Pokes, Lon chaney,

915
00:33:27,370 --> 00:33:28,130
Junior Mints.

916
00:33:28,130 --> 00:33:28,900
[CHUCKLES]

917
00:33:28,900 --> 00:33:31,710
-Uh, hello?

918
00:33:31,710 --> 00:33:32,530
Anybody work here?

919
00:33:32,530 --> 00:33:35,450
Can I get some service, please?

920
00:33:35,450 --> 00:33:37,810
-Towards the end of this
 case, Captain Lauder and I

921
00:33:37,810 --> 00:33:41,110
found out the doctor and his
 assistant had been murdered.

922
00:33:41,110 --> 00:33:43,390
But I am getting
 ahead of my story.

923
00:33:43,390 --> 00:33:43,890
-I'll say.

924
00:33:43,890 --> 00:33:46,140
-The Butcher didn't know
 how he had been brought back

925
00:33:46,140 --> 00:33:47,760
from the dead, and
 he didn't care.

926
00:33:47,760 --> 00:33:48,810
-Oh, he's under the bleachers.

927
00:33:48,810 --> 00:33:50,740
-All he knew-- he was alive.

928
00:33:50,740 --> 00:33:51,530
-Kinda.

929
00:33:51,530 --> 00:33:52,150
A little.

930
00:33:52,150 --> 00:33:54,260
-And this
 monster-made-man started

931
00:33:54,260 --> 00:33:56,840
thinking about Los Angeles.

932
00:33:56,840 --> 00:34:00,210
-"The Eyes of Laura Mars."

933
00:34:00,210 --> 00:34:02,660
Oh, I feel better now.

934
00:34:02,660 --> 00:34:05,350
Say, this whole coming
 back from the dead thing

935
00:34:05,350 --> 00:34:07,070
isn't so bad after all!

936
00:34:07,070 --> 00:34:07,570
Huh!

937
00:34:07,570 --> 00:34:09,050
Now I gotta tap a kidney.

938
00:34:09,050 --> 00:34:11,780
Be right back.

939
00:34:11,780 --> 00:34:12,970
-On that night in Los Angeles--

940
00:34:12,970 --> 00:34:13,470
-Taquitos!

941
00:34:13,470 --> 00:34:16,840
-I decided to stop playing
 detective for a little while.

942
00:34:16,840 --> 00:34:17,910
-Dude looks like a lady, huh?

943
00:34:17,910 --> 00:34:19,140
-I went to the
 burlesque house to ask

944
00:34:19,140 --> 00:34:20,470
Eva to have dinner with me.

945
00:34:20,470 --> 00:34:21,950
-I think I'm going
 to like it here.

946
00:34:21,950 --> 00:34:24,530
-After all, the Butcher was
 dead, and if I got lucky,

947
00:34:24,530 --> 00:34:26,510
sooner or later I
 figured Paul Lowe--

948
00:34:26,510 --> 00:34:27,700
[CREAKING SOUND EFFECTS]

949
00:34:27,700 --> 00:34:28,940
-Would lead me to
 the stolen payroll.

950
00:34:28,940 --> 00:34:30,930
In a way, that possibility--

951
00:34:30,930 --> 00:34:34,690
-I want everybody on stage
 for the big Whoville number!

952
00:34:34,690 --> 00:34:36,080
-Want some?

953
00:34:36,080 --> 00:34:38,340
-It's not fair to watch
 the show from backstage.

954
00:34:38,340 --> 00:34:40,150
-Next time, I'll pay admission.

955
00:34:40,150 --> 00:34:41,020
-[MAKES_RIMSHOT_NOISE]

956
00:34:41,020 --> 00:34:43,760
-I take it by that remark
 you like my routine.

957
00:34:43,760 --> 00:34:44,800
-Yeah, you're pretty good.

958
00:34:44,800 --> 00:34:45,910
-For a guy.

959
00:34:45,910 --> 00:34:47,570
-There's something,
 like, kind of spooky

960
00:34:47,570 --> 00:34:49,710
about watching Casey
 Adams flirt, isn't there?

961
00:34:49,710 --> 00:34:50,550
-Uh-huh.

962
00:34:50,550 --> 00:34:52,000
-It shouldn't be
 hard for someone

963
00:34:52,000 --> 00:34:54,830
like you to get a
 job in a class spot.

964
00:34:54,830 --> 00:34:56,510
-If you weren't a
 detective, I'd be

965
00:34:56,510 --> 00:34:57,740
willing to bet with
 a line like that,

966
00:34:57,740 --> 00:35:00,440
your next remark would
 be, "How about dinner?"

967
00:35:00,440 --> 00:35:01,140
[MAKES RIMSHOT NOISE]

968
00:35:01,140 --> 00:35:04,510
-Well, not exactly dinner, but a
 hamburger might be a good idea.

969
00:35:04,510 --> 00:35:05,580
-Ahh, meat again?

970
00:35:05,580 --> 00:35:09,310
-If you're asking in an official
 capacity, I can't say no.

971
00:35:09,310 --> 00:35:10,230
-She's got a limo.

972
00:35:10,230 --> 00:35:11,590
-I'm off duty.

973
00:35:11,590 --> 00:35:13,590
-For the past six months,
 all I've ever called you

974
00:35:13,590 --> 00:35:15,010
is Lieutenant Casey.

975
00:35:15,010 --> 00:35:16,650
Do you have a first name?

976
00:35:16,650 --> 00:35:17,600
-Mhm.

977
00:35:17,600 --> 00:35:18,100
Dick.

978
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:21,080
-That figures.

979
00:35:21,080 --> 00:35:24,190
-Give me 10 minutes for a
 quick change, because you just

980
00:35:24,190 --> 00:35:27,200
made yourself a date,
 Lieutenant-- I mean, Dick.

981
00:35:27,200 --> 00:35:28,300
-All right.

982
00:35:28,300 --> 00:35:30,460
[PURRS]

983
00:35:30,460 --> 00:35:32,170
-So why'd they go to Expo 67?

984
00:35:32,170 --> 00:35:33,680
-No, that's Brazil 76.

985
00:35:33,680 --> 00:35:34,180
-I see.

986
00:35:34,180 --> 00:35:37,180
-Well, the guy
 spares no expense.

987
00:35:37,180 --> 00:35:39,940
-I haven't had hamburger in
 the backseat of an automobile

988
00:35:39,940 --> 00:35:41,270
with a guy in a lot of years.

989
00:35:41,270 --> 00:35:42,770
-(IMITATES FEMALE VOICE)
 You're a cheap jerk.

990
00:35:42,770 --> 00:35:45,710
-From now on, we'll
 do it more often.

991
00:35:45,710 --> 00:35:47,600
-Fine.

992
00:35:47,600 --> 00:35:51,150
I always figured a policeman
 wasn't really a human being.

993
00:35:51,150 --> 00:35:53,650
I mean, unless he was
 arresting someone or trying

994
00:35:53,650 --> 00:35:55,420
to solve a case, he was unhappy.

995
00:35:55,420 --> 00:35:55,920
-That's nice.

996
00:35:55,920 --> 00:35:57,600
Pass me the jack sauce.

997
00:35:57,600 --> 00:36:00,030
-In fact, everyone on the
 force gets fed up occasionally

998
00:36:00,030 --> 00:36:01,600
with the job they do.

999
00:36:01,600 --> 00:36:03,290
-That's why we beat
 up Rodney King.

1000
00:36:03,290 --> 00:36:05,660
-Maybe we're more
 human than anyone else.

1001
00:36:05,660 --> 00:36:06,160
-Huh!

1002
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:10,120
-You see so much trouble,
 you begin to hate the world.

1003
00:36:10,120 --> 00:36:12,250
But as long as the world's
 filled with people,

1004
00:36:12,250 --> 00:36:14,710
there have to be men
 who enforce the laws.

1005
00:36:14,710 --> 00:36:16,930
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 Um, can you take me home now?

1006
00:36:16,930 --> 00:36:18,340
-What made you join
 the department?

1007
00:36:18,340 --> 00:36:19,960
-Now who's making
 with the questions?

1008
00:36:19,960 --> 00:36:20,800
-[CHUCKLES_SARCASTICALLY]

1009
00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:21,690
-No, honest.

1010
00:36:21,690 --> 00:36:23,030
I'd love to know.

1011
00:36:23,030 --> 00:36:26,000
-Well, it's really
 a very short story.

1012
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:27,120
-By Chekhov.

1013
00:36:27,120 --> 00:36:29,440
-When I got out of the Air
 Corps, I knocked around.

1014
00:36:29,440 --> 00:36:32,220
-[SIGHS_LOUDLY]

1015
00:36:32,220 --> 00:36:34,530
-Then I went to work
 for an oil company.

1016
00:36:34,530 --> 00:36:35,490
-That's why I'm so greasy.

1017
00:36:35,490 --> 00:36:38,560
-One day, I went back to
 college on the GI bill.

1018
00:36:38,560 --> 00:36:39,430
Just before graduation--

1019
00:36:39,430 --> 00:36:40,290
-[HUMS_IMPATIENTLY]

1020
00:36:40,290 --> 00:36:42,680
-I noticed all about the
 police department exams.

1021
00:36:42,680 --> 00:36:45,150
-[YAWNS_LOUDLY]

1022
00:36:45,150 --> 00:36:50,090
-[EXAGGERATED_SNORING_SOUNDS]

1023
00:36:50,090 --> 00:36:52,150
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) Can
 I throw up out the window?

1024
00:36:52,150 --> 00:36:53,110
-I have one more question.

1025
00:36:53,110 --> 00:36:54,590
Will you give me
 a straight answer?

1026
00:36:54,590 --> 00:36:56,060
-Try me.

1027
00:36:56,060 --> 00:36:59,520
-Am I still part of your
 Charles Benton investigation?

1028
00:36:59,520 --> 00:37:00,420
-And how.

1029
00:37:00,420 --> 00:37:02,690
-I mean, right now.

1030
00:37:02,690 --> 00:37:04,010
-I don't think so.

1031
00:37:04,010 --> 00:37:05,380
-(MOCKINGLY, IN UNISON)
 I don't think so.

1032
00:37:05,380 --> 00:37:07,000
-As far as the
 department's concerned,

1033
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:11,480
when Benton was executed,
 the case was marked closed.

1034
00:37:11,480 --> 00:37:13,620
If it's on to me,
 that's not good enough.

1035
00:37:13,620 --> 00:37:14,950
-[EXAGGERATED_YAWN]

1036
00:37:14,950 --> 00:37:16,280
-I don't follow.

1037
00:37:16,280 --> 00:37:19,190
-Well, the $60,000
 is still missing.

1038
00:37:19,190 --> 00:37:21,950
A fast-talking underworld
 lawyer like Paul Lowe

1039
00:37:21,950 --> 00:37:24,500
is walking around a free man.

1040
00:37:24,500 --> 00:37:25,030
-Better?

1041
00:37:25,030 --> 00:37:25,760
-Sure, I'm better.

1042
00:37:25,760 --> 00:37:27,260
-Someday, no matter
 how long it takes,

1043
00:37:27,260 --> 00:37:29,170
I'll get him and the money.

1044
00:37:29,170 --> 00:37:31,930
Until I do, the Benton case
 for me it's not closed.

1045
00:37:31,930 --> 00:37:32,860
-Oh, bravo.

1046
00:37:32,860 --> 00:37:33,360
-Yaaaay.

1047
00:37:33,360 --> 00:37:34,320
[CLAPPING AND WHISTLING]

1048
00:37:34,320 --> 00:37:35,280
-Bravo.

1049
00:37:35,280 --> 00:37:37,530
-Charles Benton never told
 me anything about the money.

1050
00:37:37,530 --> 00:37:39,690
-Well, I've been sure
 of that for a long time.

1051
00:37:39,690 --> 00:37:41,730
-Yeah.

1052
00:37:41,730 --> 00:37:43,790
-But how did you
 ever get mixed up

1053
00:37:43,790 --> 00:37:44,850
with anyone like the Butcher?

1054
00:37:44,850 --> 00:37:46,550
-(IMITATES FEMALE VOICE)
 Uh, well, I wanted a good,

1055
00:37:46,550 --> 00:37:47,400
lean brisket, and--

1056
00:37:47,400 --> 00:37:49,500
-It just happened.

1057
00:37:49,500 --> 00:37:52,630
I had a roommate who used
 to date Benton all the time.

1058
00:37:52,630 --> 00:37:55,610
One day, she packed
 and left for Chicago.

1059
00:37:55,610 --> 00:37:56,890
Benton carried
 the torch for her,

1060
00:37:56,890 --> 00:37:58,410
and he used my
 shoulder to cry on.

1061
00:37:58,410 --> 00:37:59,920
-Then he burned down the house.

1062
00:37:59,920 --> 00:38:01,620
-Next thing I knew, we were
 having dinner together.

1063
00:38:01,620 --> 00:38:03,910
And a few weeks
 later, I was labeled

1064
00:38:03,910 --> 00:38:07,980
by the police as this number
 one suspect and Benton's girl.

1065
00:38:07,980 --> 00:38:09,390
But it was never like that!

1066
00:38:09,390 --> 00:38:10,260
(YAWNING) Uh-huh, good.

1067
00:38:10,260 --> 00:38:11,580
-I made only one mistake.

1068
00:38:11,580 --> 00:38:12,970
-Excuse me, guys.

1069
00:38:12,970 --> 00:38:13,780
-[STRETCHING_SOUNDS]

1070
00:38:13,780 --> 00:38:14,280
-You mean--

1071
00:38:14,280 --> 00:38:15,700
-It's a real yawn fest.

1072
00:38:15,700 --> 00:38:17,680
-Arrested for the robbery,
 you had no idea how--

1073
00:38:17,680 --> 00:38:18,660
-Hey, hey, hey.

1074
00:38:18,660 --> 00:38:19,160
-Huh?

1075
00:38:19,160 --> 00:38:21,780
Oh, oh sorry, I just thought
 I'd stretch out a little.

1076
00:38:21,780 --> 00:38:23,870
-I want to know what you've
 got going on over here.

1077
00:38:23,870 --> 00:38:24,870
-I was just sort of looking.

1078
00:38:24,870 --> 00:38:26,740
-And all we ever talked about
 was Mags, my old roommate.

1079
00:38:26,740 --> 00:38:27,760
-No, go.

1080
00:38:27,760 --> 00:38:30,620
-Last time I saw him, he
 tried to get too friendly,

1081
00:38:30,620 --> 00:38:33,150
and that's when I told
 him he had the wrong girl.

1082
00:38:33,150 --> 00:38:34,250
-Then I had him electrocuted.

1083
00:38:34,250 --> 00:38:37,710
-Like I said, I already
 made my mistake.

1084
00:38:37,710 --> 00:38:39,710
-What about the burlesque house?

1085
00:38:39,710 --> 00:38:41,090
-Can we go back there?

1086
00:38:41,090 --> 00:38:44,280
-Please, please, when is
 this scene gonna end, please?

1087
00:38:44,280 --> 00:38:47,490
I'm getting really tired and
 cranky, and-- I don't know.

1088
00:38:47,490 --> 00:38:48,620
-And I won a beauty contest.

1089
00:38:48,620 --> 00:38:49,110
-No!

1090
00:38:49,110 --> 00:38:50,240
Not her life's story!

1091
00:38:50,240 --> 00:38:51,340
-No, please.

1092
00:38:51,340 --> 00:38:52,590
Please get rid of it.

1093
00:38:52,590 --> 00:38:53,810
Will you please?

1094
00:38:53,810 --> 00:38:54,900
-After which?

1095
00:38:54,900 --> 00:38:55,590
-Turn it off!

1096
00:38:55,590 --> 00:38:56,620
Turn it off!

1097
00:38:56,620 --> 00:38:58,020
-Really.

1098
00:38:58,020 --> 00:39:00,690
-Come on.

1099
00:39:00,690 --> 00:39:02,260
-And then one day,
 I read an ad about--

1100
00:39:02,260 --> 00:39:04,150
-Hey, have you no
 sense of decency?

1101
00:39:04,150 --> 00:39:06,080
Could you please end this scene?

1102
00:39:06,080 --> 00:39:07,180
-Yeah, jeez.

1103
00:39:07,180 --> 00:39:08,400
-And you know something?

1104
00:39:08,400 --> 00:39:10,040
It's like any other job.

1105
00:39:10,040 --> 00:39:10,800
-Yeah, where you strip.

1106
00:39:10,800 --> 00:39:12,390
-If you're looking
 for trouble, you

1107
00:39:12,390 --> 00:39:13,830
can find it even
 in the school room.

1108
00:39:13,830 --> 00:39:14,520
-Oh boy, oh boy.

1109
00:39:14,520 --> 00:39:15,250
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 That's where I hang out.

1110
00:39:15,250 --> 00:39:16,910
-Oh boy, oh boy, oh
 boy oh boy, oh boy.

1111
00:39:16,910 --> 00:39:18,180
-When the screen
 test didn't work out,

1112
00:39:18,180 --> 00:39:19,750
why didn't you go on home?

1113
00:39:19,750 --> 00:39:22,650
-Why don't you go on home
 and stop it! [VOICE_BREAKS]

1114
00:39:22,650 --> 00:39:23,390
-Joel, it's OK.

1115
00:39:23,390 --> 00:39:24,770
It's OK, honey.

1116
00:39:24,770 --> 00:39:25,270
It's OK.

1117
00:39:25,270 --> 00:39:27,480
It's OK, I'm here.

1118
00:39:27,480 --> 00:39:30,340
-I don't know about you,
 but I do eight shows a day,

1119
00:39:30,340 --> 00:39:31,930
and at this hour, I'm bushed.

1120
00:39:31,930 --> 00:39:33,010
-So do I.

1121
00:39:33,010 --> 00:39:33,800
-Oh, I see.

1122
00:39:33,800 --> 00:39:34,800
The big brush-off, eh?

1123
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:35,960
-Mind if I drive you home?

1124
00:39:35,960 --> 00:39:36,460
-No.

1125
00:39:36,460 --> 00:39:38,370
-Mighty Casey has struck out!

1126
00:39:38,370 --> 00:39:40,060
-And I'll tell you
 something else.

1127
00:39:40,060 --> 00:39:40,990
-(IN UNISON) Noooo!

1128
00:39:40,990 --> 00:39:42,700
-I haven't minded
 anything about tonight.

1129
00:39:42,700 --> 00:39:44,160
Not even the hamburgers.

1130
00:39:44,160 --> 00:39:45,620
-[INSINCERE_CHUCKLING]

1131
00:39:45,620 --> 00:39:48,070
-Kill them!

1132
00:39:48,070 --> 00:39:49,510
-I kept playing tag with Lowe.

1133
00:39:49,510 --> 00:39:51,820
He made four telephone calls.

1134
00:39:51,820 --> 00:39:53,330
Three of them
 didn't interest me.

1135
00:39:53,330 --> 00:39:54,660
But one did.

1136
00:39:54,660 --> 00:39:55,160
The one--

1137
00:39:55,160 --> 00:39:55,660
-Break, no!

1138
00:39:55,660 --> 00:39:56,370
-To Joe Marcellia.

1139
00:39:56,370 --> 00:39:57,030
-Go!

1140
00:39:57,030 --> 00:39:57,600
Go go go!

1141
00:39:57,600 --> 00:39:58,470
OK, go go!

1142
00:39:58,470 --> 00:39:58,970
Go, go!

1143
00:39:58,970 --> 00:39:59,470
Go!

1144
00:39:59,470 --> 00:40:01,520
-For some unknown reason, I
 began to get the feeling that--

1145
00:40:01,520 --> 00:40:02,020
-Go, go!

1146
00:40:02,020 --> 00:40:02,610
Go go go, go!

1147
00:40:02,610 --> 00:40:04,800
-This case was finally about
 to explode in my favor.

1148
00:40:04,800 --> 00:40:07,840
-Hey, I'm walking here.

1149
00:40:07,840 --> 00:40:09,260
-[LAUGHING]

1150
00:40:09,260 --> 00:40:10,090
-Ah!

1151
00:40:10,090 --> 00:40:13,070
-Aunt Bee, after dark.

1152
00:40:13,070 --> 00:40:14,430
-Well look at that!

1153
00:40:14,430 --> 00:40:16,700
I've been staggering
 on two legs,

1154
00:40:16,700 --> 00:40:18,890
but he's doing it on four!

1155
00:40:18,890 --> 00:40:20,260
-Try that, you flea bag!

1156
00:40:20,260 --> 00:40:21,980
-Well look who's calling names.

1157
00:40:21,980 --> 00:40:23,890
At least I ain't a squealer!

1158
00:40:23,890 --> 00:40:24,850
A stoolie!

1159
00:40:24,850 --> 00:40:25,680
-[YELLS]

1160
00:40:25,680 --> 00:40:27,340
-It's David Mamet's
 annotation of the Lockhorns!

1161
00:40:27,340 --> 00:40:28,450
-All right, Harry,
 that's enough.

1162
00:40:28,450 --> 00:40:29,400
I'll take care of them.

1163
00:40:29,400 --> 00:40:30,370
I wanna talk to them.

1164
00:40:30,370 --> 00:40:31,150
-OK, Mr. Lowe.

1165
00:40:31,150 --> 00:40:32,880
But any future business
 you've got with this psycho,

1166
00:40:32,880 --> 00:40:34,650
take care of it
 upstairs in your office.

1167
00:40:34,650 --> 00:40:36,440
He keeps showing around
 here, sooner or later,

1168
00:40:36,440 --> 00:40:38,010
he's gonna get slugged--
 cripple or no cripple.

1169
00:40:38,010 --> 00:40:39,430
-Vic Tayback, ladies
 and gentlemen.

1170
00:40:39,430 --> 00:40:43,280
[CLAPPING AND CHEERING]

1171
00:40:43,280 --> 00:40:45,570
-Ew, she's covered
 with dead things.

1172
00:40:45,570 --> 00:40:46,850
-Yuck.

1173
00:40:46,850 --> 00:40:47,710
-Go away.

1174
00:40:47,710 --> 00:40:48,890
-Let me get this straight.

1175
00:40:48,890 --> 00:40:52,350
So every table has a bottle
 of Old Smuggler on it?

1176
00:40:52,350 --> 00:40:53,190
-No.

1177
00:40:53,190 --> 00:40:54,980
-I need us sharp.

1178
00:40:54,980 --> 00:40:57,630
You'll get one,
 after I talk to you.

1179
00:40:57,630 --> 00:40:58,720
You sober enough to understand?

1180
00:40:58,720 --> 00:40:59,580
-[HICCUPS]

1181
00:40:59,580 --> 00:41:00,490
-Yeah.

1182
00:41:00,490 --> 00:41:01,440
I ran out of dough.

1183
00:41:01,440 --> 00:41:02,650
-I was going to make cookies!

1184
00:41:02,650 --> 00:41:05,370
-Look, you used to be the
 best torchman in town.

1185
00:41:05,370 --> 00:41:05,940
-Well, thank you.

1186
00:41:05,940 --> 00:41:07,070
-Now I don't think
 you could crack

1187
00:41:07,070 --> 00:41:08,810
a safe if you knew
 the combination.

1188
00:41:08,810 --> 00:41:09,330
-No jobs.

1189
00:41:09,330 --> 00:41:10,060
-Torchmen crack safes?

1190
00:41:10,060 --> 00:41:12,440
-Nobody wants me since I
 turned state's on Benton.

1191
00:41:12,440 --> 00:41:13,240
That's your doing.

1192
00:41:13,240 --> 00:41:14,060
You told me.

1193
00:41:14,060 --> 00:41:15,450
-All right, quit crying.

1194
00:41:15,450 --> 00:41:16,150
-Baby.

1195
00:41:16,150 --> 00:41:17,450
-I've got a job for you.

1196
00:41:17,450 --> 00:41:19,350
If you can stay off
 that stuff long enough--

1197
00:41:19,350 --> 00:41:20,790
-You know I can
 if I've got a job.

1198
00:41:20,790 --> 00:41:21,290
How much?

1199
00:41:21,290 --> 00:41:22,100
What is it?

1200
00:41:22,100 --> 00:41:23,580
-Pays five bottles a week.

1201
00:41:23,580 --> 00:41:24,930
-Is two grand enough for ya?

1202
00:41:24,930 --> 00:41:25,570
-Where?

1203
00:41:25,570 --> 00:41:27,520
-Right there where
 we pulled the job.

1204
00:41:27,520 --> 00:41:29,410
-Say your leg stopped dead.

1205
00:41:29,410 --> 00:41:32,300
-Two grand ain't
 much, out of $600,000.

1206
00:41:32,300 --> 00:41:33,260
-The other expenses.

1207
00:41:33,260 --> 00:41:34,600
Besides, that money's
 plenty enough.

1208
00:41:34,600 --> 00:41:36,780
-Got kind of a Bruce Willis
 smirk, there, doesn't he?

1209
00:41:36,780 --> 00:41:37,450
-Now do you want it?

1210
00:41:37,450 --> 00:41:38,230
Or do I get another?

1211
00:41:38,230 --> 00:41:38,960
-I'll do it.

1212
00:41:38,960 --> 00:41:40,720
How soon do you want
 me to go to work?

1213
00:41:40,720 --> 00:41:41,320
-Duh--

1214
00:41:41,320 --> 00:41:42,190
-Set it up tomorrow night.

1215
00:41:42,190 --> 00:41:42,930
-Let's us go.

1216
00:41:42,930 --> 00:41:44,610
-It's like a
 criminal temp agency.

1217
00:41:47,390 --> 00:41:47,890
-One.

1218
00:41:47,890 --> 00:41:50,170
And that's all till
 the job's over.

1219
00:41:50,170 --> 00:41:53,130
-Or he broke his thumb.

1220
00:41:53,130 --> 00:41:59,010
[THEME MUSIC]

1221
00:41:59,010 --> 00:42:02,520
-OK, and your hand burns,
 sending a single to your brain

1222
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:05,190
to tell you to move your
 hand off the electric heater.

1223
00:42:05,190 --> 00:42:06,830
-Oh, so in a way, pain is good.

1224
00:42:06,830 --> 00:42:09,230
Otherwise, uh, we might
 be hurt and not know it,

1225
00:42:09,230 --> 00:42:10,480
and die during social studies.

1226
00:42:10,480 --> 00:42:11,030
-Exactly.

1227
00:42:11,030 --> 00:42:12,660
It's all part of a bigger plan.

1228
00:42:12,660 --> 00:42:13,550
Get it, Crow?

1229
00:42:13,550 --> 00:42:14,600
-Yeah, I guess.

1230
00:42:14,600 --> 00:42:15,100
-OK.

1231
00:42:15,100 --> 00:42:18,340
Everybody knows there's no way a
 person could be indestructible.

1232
00:42:18,340 --> 00:42:20,670
But just for fun, what would you
 do if you were indestructible?

1233
00:42:20,670 --> 00:42:24,030
-Uhh, I'd, uh, hammer
 nails with my skull!

1234
00:42:24,030 --> 00:42:24,710
No, no wait!

1235
00:42:24,710 --> 00:42:26,760
I'd be a high performance
 drill bit, or, uh--

1236
00:42:26,760 --> 00:42:29,010
-I'd jump in a stump
 grinder to amuse my friends.

1237
00:42:29,010 --> 00:42:29,510
-Really?

1238
00:42:29,510 --> 00:42:30,500
-Oh, sure!

1239
00:42:30,500 --> 00:42:32,900
Better yet, I'd rent 15
 acres of wooded farmland

1240
00:42:32,900 --> 00:42:35,330
and invite people to hunt
 me down-- for a small fee.

1241
00:42:35,330 --> 00:42:36,450
-Oh, good one, Tommy.

1242
00:42:36,450 --> 00:42:37,460
How about this?

1243
00:42:37,460 --> 00:42:40,370
Um, I'd invite college students
 to staple announcements

1244
00:42:40,370 --> 00:42:43,090
and posters on me,
 and let them open

1245
00:42:43,090 --> 00:42:44,410
beer bottles with
 my eye sockets.

1246
00:42:44,410 --> 00:42:46,350
-Oh, then I'd allow myself
 to be used as an ashtray!

1247
00:42:46,350 --> 00:42:46,930
-Oh yeah?

1248
00:42:46,930 --> 00:42:50,090
Well, I'd wear a wool
 sweater without a t-shirt.

1249
00:42:50,090 --> 00:42:51,380
-Huh.

1250
00:42:51,380 --> 00:42:52,920
-It's Peruvian.

1251
00:42:52,920 --> 00:42:54,230
Real itchy, scratchy.

1252
00:42:54,230 --> 00:42:56,710
-But you get used
 to it after a while.

1253
00:42:56,710 --> 00:42:57,760
-Well listen, Crow.

1254
00:42:57,760 --> 00:43:00,110
Every indestructible
 fantasy does not

1255
00:43:00,110 --> 00:43:01,460
have to be gruesome or horrible.

1256
00:43:01,460 --> 00:43:02,410
-Yes it does.

1257
00:43:02,410 --> 00:43:03,620
-Sure.

1258
00:43:03,620 --> 00:43:04,390
-Uh, OK.

1259
00:43:04,390 --> 00:43:05,060
Well how about this?

1260
00:43:05,060 --> 00:43:06,710
I go down to the
 corner every day,

1261
00:43:06,710 --> 00:43:08,690
and immolate myself
 for money, kind of

1262
00:43:08,690 --> 00:43:09,840
like a street performer.

1263
00:43:09,840 --> 00:43:10,340
-Yeah!

1264
00:43:10,340 --> 00:43:10,840
-Yeah, yeah!

1265
00:43:10,840 --> 00:43:12,310
Now you're cooking with Crisco!

1266
00:43:12,310 --> 00:43:12,910
Crisco.

1267
00:43:12,910 --> 00:43:13,850
Hey, I know!

1268
00:43:13,850 --> 00:43:16,230
I'd bob for doughnuts
 in a deep fryer!

1269
00:43:16,230 --> 00:43:16,880
-I've got one.

1270
00:43:16,880 --> 00:43:18,650
I'd date Sean Young
 and then cheat on her

1271
00:43:18,650 --> 00:43:19,660
with Roseanne Barr.

1272
00:43:19,660 --> 00:43:21,350
-Oh, that's kid stuff
 compared to this.

1273
00:43:21,350 --> 00:43:22,010
Now get this.

1274
00:43:22,010 --> 00:43:24,180
I'd march right into the
 Department of Justice

1275
00:43:24,180 --> 00:43:25,520
and take the Warren
 Commission file.

1276
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:26,020
-Ooh.

1277
00:43:26,020 --> 00:43:28,230
Well, I'd go to Rome and
 unveil the conspiracy

1278
00:43:28,230 --> 00:43:29,410
between the mob and the Vatican.

1279
00:43:29,410 --> 00:43:29,950
-Oh yeah?

1280
00:43:29,950 --> 00:43:33,050
Well, I'd wear, uh, white
 tennis shoes with black socks,

1281
00:43:33,050 --> 00:43:34,790
and just wait for
 kids to beat me up.

1282
00:43:34,790 --> 00:43:36,240
[ALARM BLARES]

1283
00:43:36,240 --> 00:43:37,690
-Oh my god, movie!

1284
00:43:37,690 --> 00:43:38,190
-Whoa.

1285
00:43:38,190 --> 00:43:45,480
[DOORS CREAK]

1286
00:43:45,480 --> 00:43:48,760
-Ah, well.

1287
00:43:48,760 --> 00:43:50,680
-Meanwhile, in
 giant gila monster.

1288
00:43:50,680 --> 00:43:53,260
-I gotta take a big
 indestructible wiz.

1289
00:43:53,260 --> 00:43:54,650
-Oh, that's tasteless.

1290
00:43:54,650 --> 00:43:55,920
-Sorry.

1291
00:43:55,920 --> 00:43:59,080
-I may be indestructible,
 but I still hurt inside!

1292
00:43:59,080 --> 00:44:00,310
-[WEEPING_NOISES]

1293
00:44:00,310 --> 00:44:01,670
-I lost my retainer.

1294
00:44:05,640 --> 00:44:07,850
-Oh, they were-- they were
 thinking Julie, go away.

1295
00:44:10,450 --> 00:44:12,310
-(SINGING) Because
 I'm the wanderer,

1296
00:44:12,310 --> 00:44:14,310
they call me the wanderer.

1297
00:44:14,310 --> 00:44:17,100
-The Butcher was walking
 slowly toward Los Angeles.

1298
00:44:17,100 --> 00:44:18,260
-The killer awoke before dawn.

1299
00:44:18,260 --> 00:44:19,700
-He traveled
 through wooded hills

1300
00:44:19,700 --> 00:44:22,590
to prevent being seen by anyone.

1301
00:44:22,590 --> 00:44:24,770
Finally, he came
 to a quiet road.

1302
00:44:24,770 --> 00:44:27,040
He stopped to get his bearings.

1303
00:44:27,040 --> 00:44:28,070
-Uh, whose woods these?

1304
00:44:28,070 --> 00:44:29,720
I think no.

1305
00:44:29,720 --> 00:44:31,460
-He saw something else.

1306
00:44:31,460 --> 00:44:33,280
-Jessica Rabbit!

1307
00:44:33,280 --> 00:44:35,190
-For the Butcher,
 that rang a bell.

1308
00:44:35,190 --> 00:44:36,600
-I get it, I get it,
 I get it, I get it.

1309
00:44:36,600 --> 00:44:37,100
I do.

1310
00:44:37,100 --> 00:44:38,490
-With a car, he knew
 by the next afternoon

1311
00:44:38,490 --> 00:44:40,550
he'd be in Los Angeles
 in plenty of time

1312
00:44:40,550 --> 00:44:44,100
to catch up with the three men
 who had double-crossed him.

1313
00:44:44,100 --> 00:44:46,880
-Say, if I had a car, I could
 be in Los Angeles in time

1314
00:44:46,880 --> 00:44:48,920
to catch up to the three
 men who double-crossed me!

1315
00:44:52,540 --> 00:44:57,470
-Sooo, uhh, live
 around here much?

1316
00:44:57,470 --> 00:44:58,960
-Well, see, you're
 a real gentleman

1317
00:44:58,960 --> 00:45:00,530
to be helping
 strangers in distress.

1318
00:45:00,530 --> 00:45:02,520
I hope you don't mind me
 using the babe here as bait.

1319
00:45:02,520 --> 00:45:03,620
Quite a dish, isn't she?

1320
00:45:03,620 --> 00:45:04,120
-Hey!

1321
00:45:04,120 --> 00:45:04,950
-But that's what
 she gets paid for--

1322
00:45:04,950 --> 00:45:05,680
-I can't believe this.

1323
00:45:05,680 --> 00:45:06,410
-Babe, that is.

1324
00:45:06,410 --> 00:45:08,970
Yes sir, she's a real
 shaker of my outfit.

1325
00:45:08,970 --> 00:45:10,010
Carnival man, myself.

1326
00:45:10,010 --> 00:45:11,000
Just call me Carny.

1327
00:45:11,000 --> 00:45:11,630
-Makes me sick.

1328
00:45:11,630 --> 00:45:12,190
-Art Carney.

1329
00:45:12,190 --> 00:45:13,140
-Yeah.

1330
00:45:13,140 --> 00:45:15,580
Well, friend, we're in a
 little bit difficulty, here.

1331
00:45:15,580 --> 00:45:18,360
Got a flat tire, brand new
 car, no tools in the back.

1332
00:45:18,360 --> 00:45:19,290
Why, some of these dealers--

1333
00:45:19,290 --> 00:45:20,060
-He does go on.

1334
00:45:20,060 --> 00:45:22,060
--make my my carnival business
 look like babes in the woods.

1335
00:45:24,520 --> 00:45:25,220
-Indestructible jerk.

1336
00:45:29,720 --> 00:45:31,690
-I lo-- love this car.

1337
00:45:36,610 --> 00:45:37,600
-Huh.

1338
00:45:37,600 --> 00:45:40,300
-The time pedal, in color.

1339
00:45:40,300 --> 00:45:45,030
Duh-- Tony, where are you?

1340
00:45:45,030 --> 00:45:46,990
-My back!

1341
00:45:46,990 --> 00:45:48,310
-Say, who you with, friend?

1342
00:45:48,310 --> 00:45:48,840
And let it down!

1343
00:45:48,840 --> 00:45:49,710
Let it down.

1344
00:45:49,710 --> 00:45:50,210
-I'm with AAA.

1345
00:45:53,220 --> 00:45:55,300
-No matter who you're
 with, friend-- believe me,

1346
00:45:55,300 --> 00:45:56,440
I can do better for both of us.

1347
00:45:56,440 --> 00:45:59,060
I'll make a fortune
 for the both of us.

1348
00:45:59,060 --> 00:46:00,380
-Oops.

1349
00:46:00,380 --> 00:46:03,180
-The silent scream.

1350
00:46:03,180 --> 00:46:06,210
-Uh, I'll write up
 the contract, then.

1351
00:46:06,210 --> 00:46:07,590
-Be careful, there's
 a three on the tree,

1352
00:46:07,590 --> 00:46:08,720
and don't ride the clutch!

1353
00:46:13,530 --> 00:46:14,630
-Lie detector by Mattel.

1354
00:46:14,630 --> 00:46:17,040
-An hour later, the San
 Francisco police department

1355
00:46:17,040 --> 00:46:20,090
sent out an APB.

1356
00:46:20,090 --> 00:46:20,990
-Let me see.

1357
00:46:20,990 --> 00:46:22,570
-Big typewriters
 were brought in,

1358
00:46:22,570 --> 00:46:23,940
and people were fed into them.

1359
00:46:23,940 --> 00:46:25,740
-Squads were sent to various
 parts of the countryside.

1360
00:46:25,740 --> 00:46:28,090
-Scrods?

1361
00:46:28,090 --> 00:46:32,000
-(SINGING IN HARMONY)
 Car 54, where are you?

1362
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:33,460
-Oh, there it is.

1363
00:46:33,460 --> 00:46:36,590
-Higway patrol, starring
 Broderick Crawford.

1364
00:46:36,590 --> 00:46:38,680
-Be on the lookout
 for doughnuts.

1365
00:46:38,680 --> 00:46:39,650
-Oh, come on, Joel.

1366
00:46:39,650 --> 00:46:40,450
-APB for doughnuts.

1367
00:46:40,450 --> 00:46:41,480
-That's really lame.

1368
00:46:41,480 --> 00:46:42,860
No more of these doughnut jokes.

1369
00:46:42,860 --> 00:46:44,820
-(GIGGLES)

1370
00:46:44,820 --> 00:46:48,570
-Oh, pardon me, Tom "I've Got
 To Take An Indestructible Wiz"

1371
00:46:48,570 --> 00:46:49,070
Servo.

1372
00:46:49,070 --> 00:46:50,210
-(THREATENTING) Why, I oughta--

1373
00:46:58,260 --> 00:46:59,680
-So, the sirens of Titan, huh?

1374
00:46:59,680 --> 00:47:00,630
-Kurt Vonnegut.

1375
00:47:03,470 --> 00:47:05,350
-Oh, it's The Cannonball Run!

1376
00:47:05,350 --> 00:47:06,940
-[CACKLES]

1377
00:47:06,940 --> 00:47:08,910
-Roadblocks were thrown up to
 catch the cold-blooded killer

1378
00:47:08,910 --> 00:47:11,250
the hysterical
 girl had described.

1379
00:47:11,250 --> 00:47:12,780
-Excuse me, sir.

1380
00:47:12,780 --> 00:47:13,550
You a killer?

1381
00:47:13,550 --> 00:47:15,360
-The one the hysterical
 girl described?

1382
00:47:15,360 --> 00:47:17,440
-And each of their
 stories was the same.

1383
00:47:17,440 --> 00:47:20,270
So far, none of them had spotted
 the car the killer was driving.

1384
00:47:20,270 --> 00:47:21,180
-Sam Snead!

1385
00:47:21,180 --> 00:47:23,170
-Orders were given
 to shoot to kill.

1386
00:47:23,170 --> 00:47:25,240
-Shoot it down, shoot to kill.

1387
00:47:25,240 --> 00:47:27,300
-They realized they were
 dealing with a maniac.

1388
00:47:27,300 --> 00:47:29,850
-Who was dancing like
 he's never danced before.

1389
00:47:34,150 --> 00:47:35,580
-Whoa.

1390
00:47:35,580 --> 00:47:40,080
-Yeah, I'd like to
 see your license, sir.

1391
00:47:40,080 --> 00:47:40,620
-Oh.

1392
00:47:40,620 --> 00:47:43,100
-Take it out of
 the wallet, please.

1393
00:47:43,100 --> 00:47:45,500
Sir?

1394
00:47:45,500 --> 00:47:46,000
-Huh.

1395
00:47:49,000 --> 00:47:49,500
-[SCREAMS]

1396
00:47:49,500 --> 00:47:50,830
-Huh, mind if I play
 through? [CHUCKLES]

1397
00:47:50,830 --> 00:47:51,500
-Sam Snead!

1398
00:47:55,840 --> 00:47:58,030
-Huh, well, I suppose
 they do those things.

1399
00:48:03,110 --> 00:48:04,050
-Money.

1400
00:48:04,050 --> 00:48:05,180
Revenge.

1401
00:48:05,180 --> 00:48:06,010
Money.

1402
00:48:06,010 --> 00:48:06,840
Revenge.

1403
00:48:06,840 --> 00:48:08,080
[HONKS]

1404
00:48:08,080 --> 00:48:10,020
-Honk if you're indestructible.

1405
00:48:10,020 --> 00:48:12,250
-If you were indestructible,
 you'd be home by now.

1406
00:48:16,180 --> 00:48:22,300
-Oh, Wall Drug, 2,000 miles.

1407
00:48:22,300 --> 00:48:24,040
-Gee, if I could only read.

1408
00:48:24,040 --> 00:48:27,480
(GUFFAWS)

1409
00:48:27,480 --> 00:48:30,320
-Yeah, he's indestructible,
 but he needs a light wrap,

1410
00:48:30,320 --> 00:48:30,820
you know?

1411
00:48:30,820 --> 00:48:32,110
-Right, well the boy gets cold.

1412
00:48:32,110 --> 00:48:35,230
-More for looks.

1413
00:48:35,230 --> 00:48:36,050
-Hall of Justice.

1414
00:48:36,050 --> 00:48:39,200
Over 1,000,000 served.

1415
00:48:39,200 --> 00:48:40,680
-[IMITATES_BACKGROUND_MUSIC]

1416
00:48:40,680 --> 00:48:43,340
-Captain J. I. Estee Lauder.

1417
00:48:43,340 --> 00:48:47,100
-I trust this information will
 answer all of your questions.

1418
00:48:47,100 --> 00:48:49,560
If I can be of any further
 service, let me know.

1419
00:48:49,560 --> 00:48:51,050
-Your total is $4.35.

1420
00:48:51,050 --> 00:48:53,230
Please pull ahead.

1421
00:48:53,230 --> 00:48:55,960
-Another all-points
 from CHB, Captain.

1422
00:48:55,960 --> 00:48:57,390
-Eh, we got this
 bill from Winchell's.

1423
00:48:57,390 --> 00:49:00,140
Uh, it's in the six digits,
 so you have to sign off.

1424
00:49:00,140 --> 00:49:03,410
-Wait, who's ordering
 the specialty doughnuts?

1425
00:49:03,410 --> 00:49:04,470
Hold it a second.

1426
00:49:04,470 --> 00:49:05,190
I--

1427
00:49:05,190 --> 00:49:05,690
-Hit him.

1428
00:49:05,690 --> 00:49:07,800
-Words sound like they come
 from a bunch of loonies.

1429
00:49:07,800 --> 00:49:09,450
-Well, that's that
 Barney Miller.

1430
00:49:09,450 --> 00:49:10,300
-There it is.

1431
00:49:10,300 --> 00:49:10,950
-Sergeant.

1432
00:49:10,950 --> 00:49:11,650
-Yeah.

1433
00:49:11,650 --> 00:49:13,120
-I want Lieutenant
 Chasen to look these over

1434
00:49:13,120 --> 00:49:14,070
as soon as he comes in.

1435
00:49:14,070 --> 00:49:14,740
-All right, Captain.

1436
00:49:14,740 --> 00:49:16,110
-It's some poetry
 I've been working on.

1437
00:49:19,320 --> 00:49:20,690
-The next morning,
 I read the stories

1438
00:49:20,690 --> 00:49:23,200
of the brutal murder
 of two police officers.

1439
00:49:23,200 --> 00:49:24,250
-Really picked me up.

1440
00:49:24,250 --> 00:49:25,840
-I also read the
 witness's statement

1441
00:49:25,840 --> 00:49:28,180
about the bullets having
 no effect upon the killer.

1442
00:49:28,180 --> 00:49:30,150
-Sunny and 84 today.

1443
00:49:30,150 --> 00:49:32,170
New petitions against tax.

1444
00:49:32,170 --> 00:49:34,240
-I felt sorry for
 the witness, and I

1445
00:49:34,240 --> 00:49:37,000
thought about how easily
 a person's mind can become

1446
00:49:37,000 --> 00:49:40,700
confused when suddenly they
 receive a brutal shock.

1447
00:49:40,700 --> 00:49:41,700
-Then I went to lunch.

1448
00:49:41,700 --> 00:49:43,270
-I couldn't believe a
 man existed who could not

1449
00:49:43,270 --> 00:49:45,360
be stopped by a slug from a .45.

1450
00:49:45,360 --> 00:49:47,410
-(SINGING) Aruba, Jamaica--

1451
00:49:47,410 --> 00:49:48,180
-Good morning, Captain.

1452
00:49:48,180 --> 00:49:49,930
-Oh excuse me.

1453
00:49:49,930 --> 00:49:52,490
-You and the rest of the force
 are going on 24 hour duty.

1454
00:49:52,490 --> 00:49:54,470
-All right, yeah-- oh.

1455
00:49:54,470 --> 00:49:55,710
-Headlines?

1456
00:49:55,710 --> 00:49:57,600
-This guy that's
 running amok up north.

1457
00:49:57,600 --> 00:49:59,240
-Yeah, and he's
 running amok, too.

1458
00:49:59,240 --> 00:50:01,160
-Last report, he's killed
 two police officers.

1459
00:50:01,160 --> 00:50:03,510
Made the getaway in a
 green Coop heading south.

1460
00:50:03,510 --> 00:50:05,760
-When he gets up to four police
 officers, we'll do something.

1461
00:50:05,760 --> 00:50:07,430
-I've got a hunch this
 killer's gonna be our baby.

1462
00:50:07,430 --> 00:50:09,960
-I noticed you knitted
 a little blue holster.

1463
00:50:09,960 --> 00:50:11,240
-Yeah.

1464
00:50:11,240 --> 00:50:12,620
-Better get started, Lieutenant.

1465
00:50:12,620 --> 00:50:13,120
-OK.

1466
00:50:13,120 --> 00:50:13,620
-OK.

1467
00:50:13,620 --> 00:50:15,250
Now I gotta stop at
 Bargain Clown first.

1468
00:50:15,250 --> 00:50:17,320
-Any new leads on
 that hold-up money?

1469
00:50:17,320 --> 00:50:19,880
-I think the girl can
 probably be counted out.

1470
00:50:19,880 --> 00:50:23,150
If any of the others are wise,
 they're keep mighty quiet.

1471
00:50:23,150 --> 00:50:24,790
-Let it ride for now.

1472
00:50:24,790 --> 00:50:27,020
The whole force is
 going to be on the spot

1473
00:50:27,020 --> 00:50:28,010
until we catch this killer.

1474
00:50:28,010 --> 00:50:29,760
-They're doing "Camelot"
 across the lot.

1475
00:50:29,760 --> 00:50:30,280
-[HUMMING]

1476
00:50:30,280 --> 00:50:31,310
-Newspaper'll have a field day.

1477
00:50:31,310 --> 00:50:32,800
-Hey, shut up over there!

1478
00:50:32,800 --> 00:50:34,290
[HUMMING CONTINUES]

1479
00:50:37,270 --> 00:50:39,020
-Mm, Grandma.

1480
00:50:39,020 --> 00:50:41,240
-Hey, who says oldsters
 can't stay active?

1481
00:50:41,240 --> 00:50:42,980
-Gray lady down.

1482
00:50:42,980 --> 00:50:43,660
-All hands on deck.

1483
00:50:43,660 --> 00:50:44,190
-[WOLF_WHISTLES]

1484
00:50:44,190 --> 00:50:45,420
-We were wrong about two things.

1485
00:50:45,420 --> 00:50:47,940
The killer was
 already in our city,

1486
00:50:47,940 --> 00:50:50,550
and we didn't know he
 was Butcher Benton.

1487
00:50:50,550 --> 00:50:52,670
He headed straight
 for Eva Martin.

1488
00:50:52,670 --> 00:50:54,390
He went to her dressing
 room to get back

1489
00:50:54,390 --> 00:50:57,120
the map he had left in
 the envelope with her.

1490
00:50:57,120 --> 00:50:58,880
He wanted first to
 check his money,

1491
00:50:58,880 --> 00:51:00,200
then catch up with
 Lowe and the others.

1492
00:51:00,200 --> 00:51:01,330
-And if anybody's
 interested, he's

1493
00:51:01,330 --> 00:51:04,220
gotta pick up his shirts
 at the cleaners later, too.

1494
00:51:04,220 --> 00:51:05,760
-Hey, I'm voting in here.

1495
00:51:05,760 --> 00:51:06,890
-Oh.

1496
00:51:06,890 --> 00:51:08,620
-(SPUTTERING) But
 I was, and then,

1497
00:51:08,620 --> 00:51:10,820
you were dead with the-- ohh.

1498
00:51:10,820 --> 00:51:13,950
-Honey, I'm home.

1499
00:51:13,950 --> 00:51:15,160
-(IMITATING FEMALE
 VOICE) Oh, well.

1500
00:51:15,160 --> 00:51:17,110
-It can't be.

1501
00:51:17,110 --> 00:51:19,070
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE):
 I know I'm a little under--

1502
00:51:19,070 --> 00:51:22,780
over-- uh, I don't know
 how I'm dressed, but--

1503
00:51:22,780 --> 00:51:24,020
-You do look like him.

1504
00:51:24,020 --> 00:51:25,920
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) And
 you do smell like him, too.

1505
00:51:31,540 --> 00:51:33,010
-Carl Balentine?

1506
00:51:33,010 --> 00:51:34,440
-McHale.

1507
00:51:34,440 --> 00:51:37,780
-It is you.

1508
00:51:37,780 --> 00:51:39,070
-Why didn't somebody tell me?

1509
00:51:42,110 --> 00:51:43,740
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE)
 No, but you're not now.

1510
00:51:43,740 --> 00:51:46,660
I need to be up right now.

1511
00:51:46,660 --> 00:51:47,790
-Those look like bullet holes.

1512
00:51:47,790 --> 00:51:49,760
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) Well,
 I can patch that up easily.

1513
00:51:49,760 --> 00:51:51,120
I have a needle and thread.

1514
00:51:54,750 --> 00:51:56,520
-There's not even a mark.

1515
00:51:56,520 --> 00:51:58,450
-Hm.

1516
00:51:58,450 --> 00:52:00,380
-Darling, out of the way.

1517
00:52:06,160 --> 00:52:08,900
-Uh, I think
 scissors beats hand.

1518
00:52:08,900 --> 00:52:11,210
-Yeah.

1519
00:52:11,210 --> 00:52:12,120
-See there?

1520
00:52:12,120 --> 00:52:12,620
Look, get it?

1521
00:52:12,620 --> 00:52:13,920
No ow.

1522
00:52:13,920 --> 00:52:16,010
OK?

1523
00:52:16,010 --> 00:52:16,510
-Hmm.

1524
00:52:20,770 --> 00:52:21,520
Now watch this.

1525
00:52:21,520 --> 00:52:23,150
No paper cuts!

1526
00:52:23,150 --> 00:52:26,100
-(IMITATING FEMALE VOICE) But
 you speak in riddles, Sahib.

1527
00:52:26,100 --> 00:52:30,370
-I opened it like you told
 me, and found the $50.

1528
00:52:30,370 --> 00:52:31,220
-I had $51!

1529
00:52:31,220 --> 00:52:33,450
What happened to my dollar?

1530
00:52:33,450 --> 00:52:35,980
-I didn't know why he left it.

1531
00:52:35,980 --> 00:52:38,030
Paul didn't know
 why, and he was here!

1532
00:52:38,030 --> 00:52:39,490
-Oh, focus!

1533
00:52:39,490 --> 00:52:39,980
-Whoa.

1534
00:52:39,980 --> 00:52:40,480
-Hey!

1535
00:52:40,480 --> 00:52:41,770
-Lay's potato chips.

1536
00:52:41,770 --> 00:52:43,380
No one could eat just one.

1537
00:52:47,540 --> 00:52:51,040
-What am I doing?

1538
00:52:51,040 --> 00:52:53,030
-Don't-- don't get crazy.

1539
00:52:53,030 --> 00:52:56,670
-[GROWLS]

1540
00:52:56,670 --> 00:52:58,690
-The name of Paul
 triggered his hate.

1541
00:52:58,690 --> 00:53:00,050
-The name Gary made him happy!

1542
00:53:00,050 --> 00:53:00,700
-Go figure.

1543
00:53:00,700 --> 00:53:03,490
-Started for Squeamy Ellis
 and Joe Marcellia's place.

1544
00:53:03,490 --> 00:53:04,890
Because he knew with
 them out of the way--

1545
00:53:04,890 --> 00:53:05,090
-Hey, look.

1546
00:53:05,090 --> 00:53:06,140
There's a Spike
 Lee play showing.

1547
00:53:06,140 --> 00:53:07,480
-Paul Lowe couldn't
 open the strong box

1548
00:53:07,480 --> 00:53:10,560
where the money was hidden.

1549
00:53:10,560 --> 00:53:12,400
-Hey, I think that's
 a J. Crew barn jacket.

1550
